\par\par \b \uc2 Tanigaki: No Comment On If Changed  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Tactics  \b0\par\par\uc2 440 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 01:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  is watching the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market very closely and will act as needed to ensure \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements reflect economic fundamentals, Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said Friday.  \par \par \uc2 "Our basic stance remains unchanged," Tanigaki told reporters at a regular news conference.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki declined to comment when asked if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has shifted its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy by toning down its previous massive yen-selling.  \par \par \uc2 "I have read various news reports... but I don't comment on specific \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  techniques, such as when and under what conditions we \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki added that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements stray from economic fundamentals, but if movements are in line with fundamentals, "there is no need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said. [ 19-03-04 0043GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki's comments come amid increasing speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is scaling back its yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - a view given credence by the ministry's apparent lack of action this week to support the dollar as it plunged more than four yen.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar, which was trading at Y110.91 late Monday in Tokyo, fell as low as Y106.35 overnight in New York. At 0140 GMT, it was trading at Y107.25.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's decline overnight was in part due to a report by major Japanese daily newspaper Mainichi Shimbun saying that Tokyo is considering cutting down the scale of its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid a steadily recovering economy and growing criticism from abroad.  \par \par \uc2 Citing "international financial sources" - likely senior Japanese government officials - the report said the government has judged that the days of using massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a means to support the economy are coming to an end.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki, who acknowledged reading the report, repeated that Tokyo sticks by its stance that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates should reflect economic fundamentals.  \par \par \uc2 But in what could be a suggestion of a shift in policy, he didn't express particular concern over the rapid pace of the yen's rise in recent sessions.  \par \par \uc2 While sudden yen strength in the past has prompted Japanese policymakers to warn they would counter "speculative movements," Tanigaki didn't issue a similar threat today.  \par \par \uc2 On monetary policy, the finance minister said he welcomed Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui's "bold and drastic actions," over the past year.  \par \par \uc2 "In particular, I highly rate Governor Fukui's clear commitment to ending deflation through quantitative easing," Tanigaki said. "I would like to continue working together with Governor Fukui to overcome deflation."  \par \par \uc2 -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; miyako.takebe@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Alastair Gale and Bennett Richardson [ 19-03-04 0148GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040319e03j0000k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppi