\par\par \b \uc2 FXAnalytics Focus: MoF As Punisher  \b0\par\par\uc2 919 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 17:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By David Gilmore \par    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Analytics \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 More on what happened to MoF and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and in particular the whipping and driving of dollar/yen from Y105 to over Y112 and then subsequently pulling of bids on three different occasions.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 While not a new explanation (and one I was not ready to embrace at first), it is one that has grown in stature ... MoF as punisher. It would appear that the decision by MoF to pull bids in dollar/yen in the days that followed the March 5 (post-U.S. February payroll) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  eruption may well have been a desire to exit heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for several reasons, including protests from Greenspan and Snow, huge interest-rate and \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk for the government and people of Japan, and greater domestic scrutiny of Japan's massive accumulation of dollars. However, allegedly the decision to pull bids followed discussions with some representatives from the sell-side who presumably saw lots of the BoJ \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  flow (transact MoF/BoJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Treasury purchases). Key sell-side relationships allegedly told MoF the Monday after payrolls (March 8) the best way to reduce \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and keep dlr/yen from crumbling was to punish the speculators ... the hedge funds, overlay managers, CTA's and sell-side prop traders by yanking the bid. Keep in mind speculators were very short dollar/yen from the lows at Y105, and the MoF-driven run-up may also have been phase one of hurting speculators (this I doubt), and by Y110 most had covered and some were long, buying with MoF/BoJ. Nevertheless it is astounding that record \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (probably never to be repeated), deteriorated into a vendetta against speculators, and possibly recommended by a few from the sell-side. It is less surprising that some on the sell-side would rat out the buy-side when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flows from Japanese officials towered anything any bank has seen previously from large buy-side accounts. For better or for worse, the government of Japan for well over a year became the largest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (and fixed income) customer of more than a few sell-side institutions. Is it any wonder the government of Japan's interests would supercede the traditional buy-side client base? Indeed one would have to think that MoF had intelligence on exactly what levels specs would be forced to cover on the way up and liquidate on the way down. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculation is not for the light-hearted. Nor are there rules of engagement that MoF and its sell-side allies violated. But the actions of MoF in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in March in particular have had a far-reaching effect on liquidity, risk aversion and P&L across \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in MoF's own words is aimed at countering excessive movement. But in practice this aim was turned on its head to create excessive movement to ultimately reduce excessive movement. Furthermore it evolved into a vendetta against speculators that has left the overall \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market wounded. In the course of the last 10 weeks Japan has gone from smoothing the move down in dollar/yen, to establishing a floor at Y105, to driving the yen lower and finally to punishing speculators irrespective of level or direction. And to top it off, no one has the foggiest notion of what Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy or tactics are currently. Possibly neither does Japan. Albeit most are guessing it is back to smoothing the move down and just perhaps is ready again to prevent a move under Y105 into fiscal-year-end. And what was \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's main motivation for unleashing a tsunami of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in February and March? The Boca Raton G7 meeting when Japan was given special dispensation on the call for flexible exchange rates due to its exposure on trade in a region where \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  management is the norm. But surely G7 did not have what followed in mind when it excluded Japan in its call for greater flexibility in exchange-rate regimes. Indeed, Boca statement also said excessive volatility is undesirable ... something MoF also chose to ignore while exploiting what it came to think was a free pass on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Talk about G7 being taken for a ride ...  \par \par \uc2 As much as Japan wants a weak yen, crushing speculators is no means to an end (reducing \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ). Nor, as the move down in dollar/yen demonstrates since \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  activities were curtailed, is punishing speculators a path to stabilizing the yen at lower levels. Speculators are a key and necessary part of the financial market. They are the checks and balances on economic policy at the macro level. Without them policy makers would most assuredly have a get-out-of-jail pass to run bad policy longer. As in the case of Japan, a mercantilist-leaning economic policy would be more fully entrenched. Fortunately, the forces that attack disequilibriums and erroneous policies will regroup and prevail, especially in periods after an unimaginable extreme in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by any \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  ever before and very likely never again.  \par \par \uc2 -By David Gilmore, \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics; 860-767-9102; fxa@fxa.com  \par \par \uc2 (David Gilmore is an economist and partner at \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics, Essex, Conn., which advises institutional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market participants on a real-time basis. Neither the author nor \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics trades in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. Opinions expressed are those of the author, and not of Dow Jones Newswires.) [ 03-19-04 1111ET ]  \par \par \uc2 1057 4166  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040319e03j0001o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
