\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen extends rise vs dollar, euro, BOJ absent.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Burton Frierson  \par \uc2 684 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 13:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, March 18 (Reuters) - The yen rose to one-month highs against the dollar and six-week peaks on the euro on Thursday as foreigners bought Tokyo stocks, locals brought home overseas investments and the Bank of Japan remained conspicuously absent.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese officials warned they would act against sudden \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves but speculation \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is scaling back its massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to suppress the yen against the dollar helped the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise for the fourth day in a row.  \par \par \uc2 The Nikkei stock average hit its highest in nearly two years and data showed overseas investors continued their buying spree of Japanese stocks last week, while Japanese investors stepped up repatriation of their overseas investments last week ahead of their March 31 book-closings.  \par \par \uc2 "Everybody is speculating whether the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stopped \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  and looking at the movement in dollar/yen since Monday you would think so - or at least it hasn't been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week," said Niels Christensen, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Societe Generale.  \par \par \uc2 "With the strong performance of the Nikkei, Japanese and foreign investors are moving more funds back to Japan."  \par \par \uc2 By European midsession, the yen had risen more than 1.35 percent from its previous New York close to 106.62 per dollar, its strongest since February 19.  \par \par \uc2 The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also rose more than one percent to 130.82 yen per euro, its highest since early February. A break beyond 130.71 would be the yen's strongest since early December.  \par \par \uc2 Against the dollar, the euro rose a touch on the day to $1.2258, benefiting from the greenback's slightly steeper losses versus the yen, though it remained stuck in the broad range it has adopted since hitting a record high at $1.2927 in mid February.  \par \par \uc2 The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) meets in Frankfurt for its regular twice-monthly council meeting.  \par \par \uc2 Monetary policy is not expected to be on the agenda but some council members suggested this week they expected the economic impact of last week's Madrid bombings to be discussed.  \par \par \uc2 At 1330 GMT, economists expect weekly U.S. jobless claims to show a rise to 345,000 and producer prices to show a 0.4 percent rise. Forecasters expect a 0.1 percent rise in core producer prices.  \par \par \uc2 SWITCH OR SPOOF?  \par \par \uc2 Japanese corporations close their books for the fiscal year on March 31 and Japan is seen keen to avoid any spike up in the yen that would bite into the overseas earnings of the country's export giants.  \par \par \uc2 But speculation is growing that the country is rethinking its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy due to funding costs and possible rate hikes in the United States, which would trigger losses on Japan's growing holdings of U.S. Treasury securities.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar has fallen from a near six-month high of just over 112 yen hit on March 8 and the next level on the radar is 105.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier this week, Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  "simply out of habit", while top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat Zembei Mizoguchi reiterated on Thursday that Tokyo had not changed its stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese stocks have been rising on renewed signs of an economic upturn in the world's second-biggest economy, which is now growing faster than the United States and Europe, and further supporting the yen.  \par \par \uc2 Data showed overseas investors bought 129.6 billion yen ($1.20 billion) worth of Japanese stocks last week.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese investors also sold foreign bonds last week at their fastest pace in eight weeks and foreign equities at the fastest rate since comparable records began in 2001.  \par \par \uc2 "We had the flow data overnight, which showed strong continued evidence of inflows into Japan," said Mitul Kotecha, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Credit Agricole Indosuez.  \par \par \uc2 "We know that the speculative market has become short yen. On top of that the pullout of the Japanese authorities continues to be played on by the market. The market now is buying the yen on the back of all of these factors."   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040318e03i001yu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
