currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat Zembei Mizoguchi reiterated on Thursday that Tokyo had not changed its stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese stocks have been rising on renewed signs of an economic upturn in the world's second-biggest economy, which is now growing faster than the United States and Europe, and further supporting the yen.  \par \par \uc2 Data showed overseas investors bought 129.6 billion yen ($1.20 billion) worth of Japanese stocks last week.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese investors also sold foreign bonds last week at their fastest pace in eight weeks and foreign equities at the fastest rate since comparable records began in 2001.  \par \par \uc2 "We had the flow data overnight, which showed strong continued evidence of inflows into Japan," said Mitul Kotecha, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Credit Agricole Indosuez.  \par \par \uc2 "We know that the speculative market has become short yen. On top of that the pullout of the Japanese authorities continues to be played on by the market. The market now is buying the yen on the back of all of these factors."   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040318e03i001yr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
