\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen pushes on as BOJ remains conspicuously absent.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Charlotte Cooper  \par \uc2 669 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 10:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, March 18 (Reuters) - The yen rose for a fourth day on the dollar on Thursday, hitting a one-month high, and climbed to a six-week peak on the euro as foreigners bought Tokyo stocks and the Bank of Japan was conspicuous by its absence.  \par \par \uc2 Top Japanese officials continued to warn they would act against sudden moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market but speculation \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was scaling back its massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to suppress the yen against the dollar helped the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise to around 106.75 per dollar, its highest since mid February.  \par \par \uc2 "People are trying to interpret what has caused Japan's about-turn but it's not entirely clear what has prompted the change," said Shahab Jalinoos, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro in London.  \par \par \uc2 "The yen has had a big move from 112 to 107. If Japan at least sticks to its policy that it doesn't want to see big swings then we would expect that at some point it will re-enter the market and start \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  again."  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was more than one percent weaker on the day against the yen at 107.11 yen by 0845 GMT. The euro was 0.75 percent lower at 131.41 yen.  \par \par \uc2 Against the dollar, the euro was up about 0.3 percent at $1.2274, although still stuck in the broad range it has adopted since hitting a record high at $1.2927 in mid February.  \par \par \uc2 The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meets in Frankfurt for its regular twice monthly council meeting. Monetary policy is not expected to be on the agenda but some council members suggested this week they expected last week's Madrid bombings to be discussed.  \par \par \uc2 SWITCH OR SPOOF?  \par \par \uc2 Japanese corporations close their books for the 2003/04 fiscal year on March 31 and Japan is seen keen to avoid any spike up in the yen until then. Such a jump would bite into the overseas earnings of the country's export giants.  \par \par \uc2 But speculation is growing the country is rethinking its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy because of concerns about funding costs and possible rate hikes in the United States, which would trigger losses on Japan's growing holdings of U.S. Treasury securities.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier this week, Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  "simply out of habit", while top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat Zembei Mizoguchi reiterated on Thursday that Tokyo had not changed its stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar has fallen from a near six-month high of just over 112 yen hit on March 8 and the next level on the radar is 105, even though some expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to step in before then.  \par \par \uc2 "What's confusing is that we don't know what the authorities are trying to do," said Daisaku Ueno, head of international financial research at Nomura Research Institute in Japan.  \par \par \uc2 "First we thought they wanted to defend 105, but then they were still \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  way above that level, even above 110 as many believe."  \par \par \uc2 The yen was also supported by Japanese stocks, which have been rising on renewed signs of an economic upturn in the world's second-biggest economy, now growing at a faster clip than the United States and Europe.  \par \par \uc2 The Nikkei average surged to its highest intraday level in nearly two years on Thursday and data showed overseas investors continued their buying spree of Japanese stocks last week, purchasing 129.6 billion yen ($1.20 billion) worth.  \par \par \uc2 The Federal Reserve Bank of Philadelphia's monthly manufacturing business outlook survey is due at 1700 GMT and expected to read 30.0 for March, down from 31.4 in February.  \par \par \uc2 New jobless claims are also due and forecast to have risen to 345,000 in the week ended March 13 from 341,000 a week earlier.  \par \par \uc2 The Swiss National Bank publishes it quarterly survey at around 1300 GMT and while interest rates are forecast to remain around record lows close to zero.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040318e03i001gy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
