\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japanese officials warn  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  not over yet.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jonathan Standing  \par \uc2 578 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 09:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 18 (Reuters) - Top Japanese officials stepped up their battle of wills with \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on Thursday as the yen surged against the dollar, warning they will act against sudden moves though only when necessary.  \par \par \uc2 Speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be stepping back from aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  lifted the yen to a one-month high of 106.72 against the dollar on Thursday, some five percent above a near six-month low of around 112 yen hit on March 8.  \par \par \uc2 "We will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if needed and won't when it's not needed," Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki told a parliamentary committee, adding that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  "does not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  by aiming at specific levels".  \par \par \uc2 His deputy, Vice Finance Minister Masakazu Hayashi, chimed in by vowing to take "decisive action" against irregular \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves. He also expressed dissatisfaction with the yen's rebound in the past few days.  \par \par \uc2 "I think that recent moves have been rapid," he told reporters. "We will take decisive action against rapid and speculative moves."  \par \par \uc2 Markets are betting that Japan may be looking for a way out of an aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy that has seen it sell some 30 trillion yen ($280 billion) between January 2003 and February 2004 to keep the yen from rising.  \par \par \uc2 The speculation was triggered by a column in Tuesday's Nikkei Financial Daily that said some Bank of Japan officials thought the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would walk away from large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the end of this month, when \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fiscal year ends.  \par \par \uc2 While many were sceptical about the report, noting that the BOJ merely acts for the government, later comments by officials - including Tanigaki's remark that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if it isn't necessary - have fuelled speculation of a change.  \par \par \uc2 "The market is looking for a retreat in the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance, not an abrupt termination but a gradual retreat," said Tomoko Fujii, market and economic analysis director at Nikko Citigroup.  \par \par \uc2 Behind this thinking also lies a growing belief that Japan's recovery is strong enough to cope with a higher yen, especially as the domestic economy shows signs of improving, lessening the recovery's dependence on strong exports.  \par \par \uc2 Deflation is also slowing, easing the effects of volatile \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves on prices.  \par \par \uc2 However, the nation's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat reiterated on Thursday that there was no change in Japan's view that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates should be stable and reflect economic fundamentals.  \par \par \uc2 "Our stance has not changed," Zembei Mizoguchi, vice finance minister for international affairs, told reporters. "We continue to watch the market closely."  \par \par \uc2 Mizoguchi added that he would refrain from commenting when there were speculative moves in the market, but restated Japan's willingness to act if circumstances required, "in line with the thinking in the G7 statement in February".  \par \par \uc2 A meeting of Group of Seven finance minsters and central bankers in Florida in February agreed that excessive volatility in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets was undesirable, a view \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  saw as an understanding of its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.  \par \par \uc2 Mizoguchi, who has commented much less in his meetings with reporters in recent days than previously, declined to answer a question on recent trends in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates.  \par \par \uc2 Chief Cabinet Secretary Yasuo Fukuda also weighed into the fray on Thursday, saying Japan would act against sudden \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves.  \par \par \uc2 "If there are sudden moves in exchange rates, we will take decisive action," he told a regular news conference. ($1=107.18 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040318e03i001es\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
