\par\par \b \uc2 Yen surges against dollar on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  test.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By David McMahon  \par \uc2 637 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 07:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, March 18 (Reuters) - The yen surged to a one-month high against the dollar on Thursday as foreigners snapped up Tokyo stocks and speculation continued that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be stepping back from aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  The yen jumped to around 106.72 per dollar above the New York high of 107.95 yen and the highest since February 19.   \par \par \uc2  By 0612 GMT, it had settled around 107.10 yen, up a full yen on the day.   \par \par \uc2  Riding the same wave, the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also surged against the euro to a fresh six-week high of 131.11 yen.   \par \par \uc2  Traders said there was a good chance the dollar would fall to its next support level of 105 yen in the near term, as the market gingerly tests the resolve of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Ministry to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  "What's confusing is that we don't know what the authorities are trying to do," said Daisaku Ueno, head of international financial research at Nomura Research Institute.   \par \par \uc2  "First we thought they wanted to defend 105, but then they were still \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  way above that level, even above 110 as many believe."   \par \par \uc2  The dollar has fallen almost five percent from a near six-month high of just over 112 yen hit on March 8.   \par \par \uc2  The yen was also supported by a healthy appetite for Japanese stocks, which have been rising on renewed signs of an economic upturn in the world's second-biggest economy - which is now growing at a faster clip than the United States and Europe.   \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei average surged to its highest intraday level in nearly two years on Thursday, rising by as much as 1.84 percent to 11,647.71 in early trade. Market players suspected foreigners' stock buying was driving the rally.   \par \par \uc2  According to government data released on Thursday, overseas investors continued their buying spree of Japanese stocks last week, purchasing 129.6 billion yen ($1.20 billion) worth.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  OVER?   \par \par \uc2  Speculation was growing that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is rethinking its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy because of concerns about funding costs and possible rate hikes in the United States, which would trigger losses on Japan's growing holdings of U.S. Treasury securities.   \par \par \uc2  Earlier this week, Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  "simply out of habit", while top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat Zembei Mizoguchi reiterated on Thursday that Tokyo had not changed its stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates.   \par \par \uc2  Analysts though, say the tide is slowly turning.   \par \par \uc2  "The market is looking for a retreat in the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance, not an abrupt termination but a gradual retreat," said Tomoko Fujii, market and economic analysis director at Nikko Citigroup.   \par \par \uc2  Japanese corporations close their books for the 2003/04 fiscal year on March 31, and Japan is seen keen to avoid any spike in the yen until then. Such a jump would bite into the overseas earnings of the country's export giants.   \par \par \uc2  The yen also rose against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as sterling, and analysts cited unwinding of high yielding foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  denominated bonds for repatriation ahead of book closing.   \par \par \uc2  The euro was trading at $1.2283, a little above late New York levels.   \par \par \uc2  In U.S. trading, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slipped to a low of $1.2176 pressured by a steep rise in the yen versus the euro.   \par \par \uc2  The market was also anticipating an announcement following a general council meeting of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to be held in Frankfurt later in the day, to gauge European sentiment following last week's bombings in Madrid.   \par \par \uc2  Dealers will also have an eye on UK retail sales data for February, which may give them a clue as to when the Bank of England will decide to raise its official interest rate from four percent to cool a fast-growing economy. ($1=108.21 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040318e03i00136\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
