\par\par \b \uc2 Yen steady vs dollar but more gains expected.  \b0\par\par\uc2 455 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 02:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 18 (Reuters) - The yen took a breather on Thursday after rallying to fresh highs versus the dollar and the euro the previous day, but traders said it was only a matter of time before the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  made further gains.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said the yen's upward trend would continue given the current strength of Tokyo stocks and on speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be stepping back from aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 At 0046 GMT, the dollar had settled around 108.20 yen, little changed from late U.S. levels but up from the New York trough of 107.95, the lowest in nearly four weeks.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said they were prepared to buy the yen further on possible signs that the Finance Ministry could be reducing its yen-selling.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier this week, Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  "simply out of habit", while Haruhiko Kuroda, special adviser to Japanese Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi, pointed out that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was only a short-term tool.  \par \par \uc2 "The tone coming from Mr. Tanigaki and Mr. Kuroda was different from what we've been hearing, and (U.S. Treasury Secretary John) Snow declined to comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Junya Tanase, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan Chase.  \par \par \uc2 "These elements are shifting the yen's direction, at least in the short term."  \par \par \uc2 Snow was interviewed on CNBC television on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the euro held ground against the yen after dropping to a six-week low of 131.70 yen in New York trading. It was sitting at 132.56 yen, hardly changed from overnight levels.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was trading at $1.2251, slightly above late New York levels. In U.S. trading, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slipped to a low of $1.2176 pressured by a steep rise in the yen versus the euro.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the market would be focusing on how yen-cross trading would affect the dollar/yen as the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was likely to remain supported on recent strength in Tokyo shares.  \par \par \uc2 The Nikkei average surged to its highest level in nearly two years on Thursday, rising 1.84 percent to 11,647.71 in early trade. Market players suspected foreigners' stock buying was driving the rally.  \par \par \uc2 According to government data released on Thursday, overseas investors continued their buying spree of Japanese stocks last week, purchasing 129.6 billion yen ($1.20 billion) worth.  \par \par \uc2 Against sterling, the yen was trading at 196.53 per pound near New York highs.  \par \par \uc2 The market was also anticipating an announcement following a general council meeting of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to be held in Frankfurt later in the day, to gauge European sentiment following last week's bombings in Madrid. ($1=108.21 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040318e03i0003e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
