\par\par \b \uc2 Mizoguchi says no change in Japan's  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  stance.  \b0\par\par\uc2 142 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 01:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, March 18 (Reuters) - Top financial diplomat Zembei Mizoguchi said on Thursday there is no change in Japan's stance that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates should be stable and reflect economic fundamentals.   \par \par \uc2  "Our stance has not changed," Mizoguchi, vice finance minister for international affairs, told reporters at the ministry. "We continue to watch the market closely."   \par \par \uc2  He added that he would refrain from commenting when there were speculative moves in the market.   \par \par \uc2  The yen hit a three-week high versus the dollar and a six-week peak against the euro on Wednesday amid rising speculation \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may reduce its huge yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which acts on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has been selling yen for dollars to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  so as to support exporters.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040318e03i00028\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
