\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 1-Stocks, oil jump - bonds ease.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Gary Hill  \par \uc2 886 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 01:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, March 16 (Reuters) - U.S. stocks rallied on Wednesday as a benign inflation report fed economic optimism that had already been stoked the day before by a signal of no imminent rise in interest rates, but oil prices climbed on the threat of a supply crunch that could hurt economic growth.  \par \par \uc2 Oil prices hit their highest closing level in more than 13 years as a drop in already low U.S. gasoline inventories sharpened the threat of shortages.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury prices eased as mild profit-taking and the rebound in stocks put a minor dent in recent gains.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar fell against the yen but rose against the euro amid speculation \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may curb its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a time when its stock market is attracting more cash.  \par \par \uc2 On Wall Street, the blue-chip Dow posted its biggest daily percentage gain in five weeks after the government said U.S. consumer price increases slowed in February as energy costs moderated. In addition, companies including FedEx Corp. (FDX.N) reported solid earnings.  \par \par \uc2 Investors were already encouraged about the economy after comments from the Federal Reserve on Tuesday, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  signaled it is in no hurry to raise interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 The Fed's comments soothed jittery investors after a few sessions of losses, roiled by last week's Madrid bombings.  \par \par \uc2 "The news from the Fed was viewed as positive, and the market was looking for an excuse to go up. Today's economic data also took out some inflation fear," said Michael Murphy, a managing director at Wachovia Securities in Baltimore.  \par \par \uc2 Despite the market's rally, volume was relatively restrained, Murphy said. "You saw a lot of volume on the downside, and today it's very quiet on the upside."   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Dow Jones industrial average  rose 115.63 points, or 1.14 percent,\par to 10,300.30, its biggest percentage gain since Feb. 11. The Standard &\par Poor's 500 Index        gained 13.05 points, or 1.17 percent, to\par 1,123.75. The Nasdaq Composite Index         added 33.67 points, or\par 1.73 percent, to 1,976.76.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FedEx, the world's leading air-express group, reported a better-than-expected rise in quarterly earnings, and said it was feeling the effects of strengthening economies around the world. Its shares rose $3.16, about 4.6 percent, to $71.59.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Internet media company Yahoo Inc. (YHOO.O) bolstered the tech-heavy Nasdaq, after a bullish call on its stock from Smith Barney. Yahoo rose $2.28, or nearly 5.4 percent, to $44.85 after the brokerage upgraded the shares to "buy" from "hold," citing strong cash flows from Yahoo-branded Web sites.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But Agere Systems Inc. (AGRa.N) fell, after warning that its quarterly sales and earnings before a tax benefit would fall short of forecasts, due to weak sales of chipsets for advanced mobile phones. Shares of Agere, a maker of microchips for communications systems, fell 18 cents, or nearly 5.3 percent, to $3.23 and topped the New York Stock Exchange's most actively traded list.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In oil markets, U.S. light crude futures rose 70 cents to settle at $38.18 a barrel, nearly 2 percent higher and the highest settlement for U.S. crude since October 1990.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Prices jumped after the government reported a further decline of 800,000 barrels in gasoline stocks to 199.6 million barrels.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. gasoline supplies are running 5 percent below the five-year average, sparking concerns refineries will struggle to build supplies in time for summer holiday driving demand.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bond prices eased after a two-week run-up that began after last month's disappointing U.S. jobs figures.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The benchmark 10-year note slipped 7/32 in price and was yielding 3.71 percent.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Five-year notes  fell 4/32 for a yield of 2.67 percent from 2.64\par percent, while the 30-year bond             lost 13/32 for a yield of\par 4.67 percent from 4.64 percent.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen hit a three-week high versus the dollar and a six-week peak against the euro amid rising speculation \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may reduce its yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at a time when its stock market is attracting more cash.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar fell 0.61 percent to 108.21 yen . The euro fell against the dollar to $1.2235 , weighed by the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's losses against the yen, traders said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 COMEX gold escaped a tight range late in the day after an explosion at a Baghdad hotel killed at least 25 people. Investors seeking the safe-haven metal helped drive April gold up $4.50 to $407.10 an ounce, near the high of its $401.90-$407.50 range.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Earlier, the London stock market had its strongest session in nearly two weeks after bullish forecasts for British economic growth. The FTSE 100 closed up 27.9 points, or about 0.6 percent, at 4,456.8 for its biggest rise since March 4.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Tokyo, the Nikkei average jumped 1.73 percent to 11,436.86. Its biggest percentage gain since March 1 was fueled by growing optimism over Japan's economic recovery. (Reporting by Vivian Chu, Pedro Nicolaci da Costa, Daniel Bases, Andrew Mitchell, Alden Bentley).   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Doku