\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Speculators, Like BOJ, Absent In Dollar Fall  \b0\par\par\uc2 913 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 21:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Tom Barkley \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The dollar has gone almost full circle against the yen over the past two months, from three and a half year lows just above Y105 in mid-February to above Y112 and nearly back all the way down again in the past two weeks.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 With Japanese monetary authorities apparently flipping the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  switch on and off again like a mischievous prankster, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  investors are left to speculate what may be driving the seeming shifts in policy - and where it may be heading next.  \par \par \uc2 In fact, keeping investors guessing may be the aim of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Analysts point to the notable lack of participation of speculators - typically hedge funds or others who take a shorter-term view of the market - in the dollar's slide of over 5% from a 5 1/2-month high of Y112.35 a little over two weeks ago to as low as Y106.43 on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 "Speculators aren't really taking on the BOJ and trying to push it lower," said Greg Anderson, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro Bank in Chicago. "They've been burned so many times in the last three weeks that they don't dare touch it."  \par \par \uc2 ABN Amro's positioning indicator, which captures interbank market data, has shown "essentially zero participation" among speculators from the mostly U.S. and some European investors that it tracks, said Anderson. He says this suggests flows into the yen have come mostly from Asia and "probably aren't speculative."  \par \par \uc2 According to the indicator, investors were still holding modestly long positions in the dollar agains the yen, essentially making a small bet on dollar gains - up until Thursday's break below Y107, indicating a continued reluctance to short the yen due to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  concerns, he added.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par       Textbook Result \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Positioning data from the International Monetary Market on the Chicago \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  futures exchange shows a similar lack of enthusiasm among speculators to bet on a dollar decline. In the five weeks to March 9, the speculative community swung from a massive net long yen position to the largest net long dollar position since August 2003, according to Citigroup. Though the bank's analysts cited anecdotal evidence of heavy yen buying this week, that will likely be reflected in later IMM reports.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Essentially then, despite the sharp climb in the yen, Japan's Finance Ministry has achieved one of the stated goals of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  - curtailing speculative bets on the yen. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is by far the largest practitioner of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . It sells its own \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stop a strong yen from derailing its export-led recovery and typically parks the proceeds of those sales - mostly for dollars - in U.S. assets, mainly Treasurys bonds.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on behalf of the ministry, had helped push the dollar back up to Y112 in an apparent ratcheting up of tactics by buying dollars on rallies. Now that speculators have been temporarily sidelined, some analysts believe the BOJ will go back to its previous strategy of smoothing the yen's appreciation.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "One of the interesting reasons I think the MOF feels comfortable to let the yen rise at this point is that there's not much of a speculative move behind it," said Jin Saito, Japan analyst at the G7 Group, an investment advisory firm in Washington. "Yen bulls were so badly hurt after the mid-February (long yen) unwinding took place, that I don't think there's any momentum behind this \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  move."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Instead, the yen's surge now has been driven largely by heavy portfolio flows into Japan - over Y3 trillion in net inflows in the two weeks ended March 12 - and the absence of official BOJ bids. Repatriation ahead of Japan's March 31 fiscal year-end is believed to account for some of that flow, though most of that activity has likely been wound up by now, say analysts.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, if the BOJ were to come back in, Saito reckons the dollar could quickly rebound two yen due to a lack of conviction in the current move.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     Positioning Looks Extreme \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Increasing wariness about \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as the dollar once again approaches Y105 has caused a number of analysts to urge caution. Citigroup recommended taking profits on a short position in the dollar against the yen it established on Friday, saying that the "risk/reward profile" had "shifted dramatically."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The bank's analysts expressed doubts about whether Japan's monetary authorities would be likely to ease off completely given the record levels of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in 2003 and the first two months of this year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the options market, the level of protection being sought against a further drop in the dollar against the yen has also reached an extreme, signaling a potential market reversal.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Marc Chandler, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at HSBC Bank in New York, said in a research note that such extreme readings in the options market - including a surge in dollar/yen volatilities - tended to be associated with \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and could indicate that that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may return with more of a force. He suggested "some profit-taking may be prudent."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Tom Barkley is a special writer covering \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in New York. He previously covered convertible and high-yield debt markets.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Tom Barkley, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4385  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     tom.barkley@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 03-18-04 1538ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040318e03i0003w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
