\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Options - BOJ speculation spurs dollar put demand.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kyle Peterson  \par \uc2 641 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 21:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 CHICAGO, March 17 (Reuters) - Heightened speculation that Japanese monetary authorities soon may stop buying dollars has triggered aggressive demand in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  options market for options to sell the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The dollar has shed more than 2 percent against the yen since Monday when a report appeared in Tuesday's edition of the Nihon Keizai Shimbun saying some officials within the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will abandon its yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  beginning in April.  \par \par \uc2 The newspaper report spurred new demand for dollar puts and yen calls because it consolidated the market's view that \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be watered down and soon, analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 Puts give the holder the right but not the obligation to sell the underlying \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  if it reaches a predetermined trigger price. A call gives the holder the right to buy the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Such talk did not take investors completely by surprise. Market watchers have for weeks predicted Japanese financial authorities, who have been buying dollars for yen in record amounts this year, will tone down their yen-weakening market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the Japanese fiscal year ends on March 31.  \par \par \uc2 "There have been nothing but yen call buyers now after the new report about the BOJ toning down the massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign after fiscal year end," said Enrico Caruso, vice president and chief dealer at Tempest Asset Management in Irvine, California. "The market has definitely gotten on board the 'kindler, gentler' BOJ notion at month's end."  \par \par \uc2 Analysts reckon Japan hopes to keep the dollar above 105 yen at least until Japanese companies report their fiscal year-end results.  \par \par \uc2 A sharply stronger yen could skew Japanese earnings to the downside for some international corporations based in Japan. That, in turn, could weaken the Japanese stock market, these analysts say.  \par \par \uc2 The same analysts also believe Japan will remove that support after the fiscal year end, a position bolstered by the Japanese press.  \par \par \uc2 DOLLAR DESCENT CAUSED WORRIES  \par \par \uc2 Since starting its steep downtrend in February 2002, the dollar has fallen nearly 20 percent against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , causing many Japanese leaders to worry about the impact of the rising yen on the recovering local export market.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar has sold off sharply in the first three days of this week, however, as traders braced for likely lower dollar levels in the next fiscal year once support from the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  is removed.  \par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the dollar bought about 108.10 yen , down from 112.32 yen on March 8.  \par \par \uc2 A month ago, the dollar hit three-year lows against the yen just above 105 yen.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts linked the dollar's abrupt move higher to efforts by the BOJ, which in January and February bought half of the annual record set in 2003 of 20 trillion yen ($180 billion).  \par \par \uc2 "I think toward the end of today people were realizing that we're in a different world than we were last week," said Simon Smollett, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options analyst at IDEAglobal in London. "I think the new approach is to let it (the dollar) down gently. We might be going to 105 or 106 but not in a few days."  \par \par \uc2 The one-month risk reversal, which can be a measure of the market's preference for puts against calls, was near 1.8 percent on Wednesday favoring yen calls, after favoring yen calls by just 0.4 percent on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 A week ago, the risk reversal favored yen puts.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts noted no major shifts in general options market strategies this week, although both Caruso and Smollett reported some interest in options with strikes in the 108 yen area.  \par \par \uc2 Below 105 yen, however, lurks a reported mine field of options strikes and dollar sell orders that threaten to send the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as low as 95 yen.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040317e03h002vu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
