\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Steady Fed buoys stocks, bonds - dents dollar.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Nigel Stephenson  \par \uc2 623 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 15:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, March 17 (Reuters) - Clear signals the U.S. is in no hurry to raise the cost of borrowing helped boost stocks, sent government debt prices soaring and dented the dollar on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 The Federal Reserve's Open Market Committee held rates at a 1958 low of 1.00 percent and again said it could afford to be "patient" about raising rates despite a strengthening economy because inflation was muted and jobs growth weak.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks looked set to build on Tuesday's gains. They see-sawed after the decision, before rising on optimism that credit costs would stay low. European shares also pushed higher.  \par \par \uc2 Sharp rises in U.S. Treasury prices were followed by gains in euro zone government bonds.  \par \par \uc2 "It is now absolutely clear there will be no rate hike in June and most probably the Fed will stay on hold until after the (U.S. presidential) election," said Kornelius Purps, fixed income strategist at HypoVereinsbank in Munich.  \par \par \uc2 Doubts about the strength of recovery in the world-leading U.S. economy had hit stocks in recent weeks and this was exacerbated by last Thursday's deadly bomb attacks in Madrid.  \par \par \uc2 However, while economic weakness could hit corporate profits, lower borrowing costs would be a boon.  \par \par \uc2 European shares rose, lifted mainly by robust company results.  \par \par \uc2 Chemicals group BASF rose more than three percent after it said its quarterly profit rose more than 25 percent and reinsurer Munich Re was up 2.9 percent after results.  \par \par \uc2 The FTSE Eurotop 300 index of pan-European blue chips was up 0.93 percent and the narrower DJ Euro STOXX 50 index was up 1.22 percent.  \par \par \uc2 "We feel that the mild up-trend is still intact but markets are likely to move around current levels for a while," said Lex Werkheim of Eureffect asset management in Amsterdam.  \par \par \uc2 RECOVERY SIGNALS BOOST TOKYO STOCKS  \par \par \uc2 Earlier, growing optimism that Japan's period of deflation is ending helped lift Tokyo's TOPIX index to a 2-1/2-year high.  \par \par \uc2 The TOPIX rose 1.85 percent to finish at its highest close since August 28, 2001. The benchmark Nikkei average gained 1.73 percent.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. stock index futures were higher, indicating Wall Street was likely to open higher.  \par \par \uc2 Euro zone government bond yields, which move inversely to prices, fell sharply. The two-year Schatz yield was down 4.5 basis points (bps) at 2.12 percent, having fallen as far as 2.088 percent - its lowest since June 2003.  \par \par \uc2 The benchmark 10-year Bund yield was at 3.88 percent, down 5.1 bps.  \par \par \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief economist Otmar Issing was quoted by a German magazine as saying the Madrid bombings were hurting economic confidence but lowering rates would not help sentiment.  \par \par \uc2 With no dollar-boosting rate rise in sight, the yen surged against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on speculation \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may scale back its yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The yen pushed to three-week highs against the dollar around 108.08 and was last at 108.15. Against the euro, the yen rose to a six-week high around 132 and was last at 132.23. The euro was last at $1.2227.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the yen's gains were accelerated by remarks from former top finance official Eisuke Sakakibara, who told Reuters it was necessary for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to exit its policy of massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at some point.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling was flat at $1.82, unmoved by the UK budget which was presented to parliament.  \par \par \uc2 Gold, whose rise this week was boosted by security concerns, held steady. Spot gold was last at $402.80/403.60, compared with $402/402.80 in New York late on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040317e03h001f5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
