\par\par \b \uc2 JGBs sag on Nikkei rally, auction jitters.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Shinichi Kishima  \par \uc2 681 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 08:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 17 (Reuters) - Japanese government bonds weakened on Wednesday as a two percent rally in Tokyo stocks sapped investors' appetite for bonds, which was already less than vigorous a day ahead of a 20-year JGB auction.  \par \par \uc2 Although JGBs drew some support from Tuesday's gains in U.S. Treasuries in the wake of a Federal Reserve statement that sounded a cautionary note on the dormant U.S. jobs market, stocks' bullishness pushed bond trading into a defensive mode.  \par \par \uc2 The yield on the current benchmark 20-year JGB rose 2.0 basis points to 1.885 percent. This virtually ensured that the coupon on Thursday's new offering would be 1.9 percent - a notch above last month's 1.8 percent.  \par \par \uc2 "At these levels, the market has seen bargain hunters return on a number of occasions recently, but the timing is not good," said Jun Ishii, chief fixed income strategist at Mitsubishi Securities.  \par \par \uc2 "Besides the end of the business year (on March 31), we have a lot of risk events in early April. People are liable to take a more cautious approach."  \par \par \uc2 The 500 billion yen ($4.6 billion) 20-year offering on Thursday is the last long-dated JGB to be auctioned before the fiscal year-end.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan releases its quarterly "tankan" business survey on April 1, while the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (MOF) plan to to increase its monthly issuance of 20-year JGBs to 600 billion yen next month is fuelling supply concerns.  \par \par \uc2 The longer end of the yield curve led the losses on Wednesday, with the 30-year yield rising 3.5 bps to 2.210 percent and the five-year yield rising 1.5 bps to 0.530 percent.  \par \par \uc2 The yield on the bellwether 10-year JGBs rose 2.5 bps to 1.310 percent, with June 10-year bond futures falling 0.20 to 139.03 on modest turnover of 18,682 contracts.  \par \par \uc2 "There is pressure on the curve to steepen ahead of the 20-year JGB auction tomorrow," said Tetsuya Kumata, an investment analyst at Mizuno Securities.  \par \par \uc2 "With the issue amount up 100 billion yen from next month and ahead of book-closings, investors may well think there is no reason to rush to buy."  \par \par \uc2 NIKKEI RALLY  \par \par \uc2 With a Tokyo stock market rally inspired by optimism over a domestic economic recovery - construction firms, real estate and banks leading the gains - renewed strength in the yen proved to be of little help for JGBs, Ishii added.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The stock market's benchmark Nikkei average  rose more than two percent\par at one point to close above 11,400 for the first time in a week, while\par the broader TOPIX index         gained 1.85 percent to close at a 2-=\par year high.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar fell more than half a yen to three-week lows around 108.20 yen amid a growing belief that Japanese authorities would not be able to maintain their heavy yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which swelled to a record 20 trillion yen last year and has already reached 10 trillion yen in just the first two month of this year, has resulted in an increased issuance of three-month finance bills (FBs).  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Wednesday, the MOF offered 5.145 trillion yen of FBs through an auction, which produced a stop rate of 0.0158 percent - up from 0.0119 percent in an auction last week.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Although the BOJ's relaxed monetary policy, which was left unchanged at a meeting that ended on Tuesday, has kept short-term rates near zero for the last few years, the market is increasingly worried about long-term rates as Japan's public-sector debt - already 140 percent of gross domestic product - keeps growing.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts say such concerns, exacerbated by year-end wariness, have been a major reason behind poorly received auctions of 10-and 30-year offerings late last month which caused an across-the-board bond market selloff. ($1=108.81 yen) (Additional reporting by Hideyuki Sano).   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040317e03h000xm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
