\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar hits 3-week lows vs yen on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  doubts.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By David McMahon  \par \uc2 642 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 07:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, March 17 (Reuters) - The dollar slid to new three-week lows against the yen on Wednesday as traders wondered if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would scale-back its massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and as the prospect of interest rate hikes in the U.S. faded.   \par \par \uc2  Speculation was building that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be rethinking its policy of dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid worries about losses on the world's largest \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, much of which are ploughed into the U.S. Treasury market.   \par \par \uc2  Traders said the fall was accelerated by remarks from former top finance official Eisuke Sakakibara, who told Reuters that it was necessary for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to exit its policy of massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at some point.   \par \par \uc2  "There's been a huge change in market sentiment in the last week about \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy," said Jake Moore, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Barclay's Bank.   \par \par \uc2  Traders said that investors had reacted to comments by Sakakibara that the United States would not tolerate yen-selling to push the yen below 115 yen.   \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell to around 108.25 yen for the first time in three weeks and Tokyo traders said it could soon test waters below 108 yen. It was around 108.80 yen in late New York trade on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2  The yen also edged up against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , touching a one-month high around 132.55 per euro   \par \par \uc2  Traders said the euro was sluggish amid concerns about Europe's economy and heightened security worries after a major French newspaper published a letter from a fringe Islamic group threatening to "plunge France into terror and remorse".   \par \par \uc2  Investors were awaiting euro zone consumer prices data due at 1000 GMT, a day after Germany's ZEW institute said its gauge of economic expectations fell in March for the third straight month to its lowest level since last summer.   \par \par \uc2  Weaker-than-expected prices data could rekindle market expectations about a rate cut by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  By 0627 GMT, the euro was trading around $1.2270 hardly changed from its late New York level.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  INTRIGUE   \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara's comments came after Haruhiko Kuroda, a special adviser to Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi, said on Tuesday that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a short-term policy tool that cannot truly manipulate the world's three major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  because of vast global capital flows.   \par \par \uc2  Japan's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat Zembei Mizoguchi reiterated on Wednesday that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to dampen volatility.   \par \par \uc2  But traders said that even though \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  looked unsustainable in the longer run, few were ready to aggressively sell the dollar yet.   \par \par \uc2  "Even if there were to be a change in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy it would likely be continued until the end of March," said Mitsuru Sahara, vice president of the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealing group at UFJ Bank.   \par \par \uc2  Japanese corporations close their books for the 2003/04 fiscal year on March 31, and Japan is seen keen to avoid any spike in the yen until then. Such a jump would bite into the overseas earnings of the country's export giants.   \par \par \uc2  Also weighing on the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the prospect that the U.S. Federal Reserve was in no hurry to raise interest rates anytime soon.   \par \par \uc2  In a statement following its meeting on Tuesday, the Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC) expressed more caution on the economy, noting continued weakness in job creation, which dulled the prospects of higher rates any time soon.   \par \par \uc2  Higher rates would make dollar-based assets more attractive to foreign investors. The benchmark official U.S. lending rate stands at 1 percent, the lowest since 1958, compared with 4.0 percent in Britain.   \par \par \uc2  Fed Chairman Alan Greenspan is due to speak via satellite on banking to the Independent Community Bankers of America Convention in San Diego at 1630 GMT.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040317e03h000sc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
