\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW- \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  eyes exit strategy on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 -Sakakibara.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Linda Sieg   \par \uc2 613 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 04:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, March 17 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is looking for ways to exit its policy of massive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but will have to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  at times to keep speculators from driving up the yen, former top finance official Eisuke Sakakibara said on Wednesday.    \par \par \uc2  "Massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly during the course of the last two months, cannot be continued forever so some kind of exit policy is necessary," Sakakibara, once dubbed "Mr Yen" for his influence in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, told Reuters.    \par \par \uc2  "When it would come, how it would come, is something which is very difficult to predict," he said.    \par \par \uc2  "Exit policy from fixed exchange rates or massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is very difficult, so it is a major task for Mr Mizoguchi and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but I am sure they are considering it," he added, referring to Zembei Mizoguchi, Japan's vice finance minister for international affairs.    \par \par \uc2  Japan, worried that a strong yen could damage its export-led economic recovery, sold a record 20 trillion yen ($184 billion) in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market last year and a total of more than 10 trillion yen in January and February.    \par \par \uc2  But speculation is growing that Japan is rethinking its policy because of concerns about exposure to domestic interest rate rises, which would drive up funding costs, as well as worries about exchange rate losses on ballooning holdings of U.S. Treasury securities resulting from dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .    \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also been criticised for keeping its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  artificially weak, giving it an unfair trade advantage.    \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara, who left the finance ministry in 1999, said Japanese authorities had successfully capped the yen's rise at 105 yen to the dollar through persistent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and when the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell to 107 they had skilfully assisted its further decline by selling yen.    \par \par \uc2  He said, however, that economic fundamentals on both sides of the Pacific meant the yen would appreciate and that U.S. authorities were not likely to sign off on Japanese market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that sought to push the yen below 115.    \par \par \uc2  MORE TOLERANCE FOR STRONG YEN    \par \par \uc2  "The trend of the U.S. and Japanese economies at the moment is toward the appreciation of the yen," Sakakibara said, noting that the long-awaited Japanese recovery appeared to be real and sustainable, while the U.S. economy might be nearing its peak.    \par \par \uc2  "For the next two-three months, I think what would happen is a slow appreciation toward 105," he said.    \par \par \uc2  "I think the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  would still \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , maybe not that aggressively, but defensive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is there, so I would not predict any sharp fall of the U.S. dollar, but some gradual sort of appreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  toward 105 is something we will probably observe," he said.    \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell to a three-and-a-half year low of about 105 yen last month, down about 20 percent in two years. But it rose by six percent to above 112 yen last week, helped by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It was trading around 108.75 yen on Wednesday.    \par \par \uc2  A growing belief among Japanese officials that the nation's recovery is sustainable means authorities can now tolerate a somewhat stronger yen, Sakakibara said.    \par \par \uc2  But if the market overshot and there was a sudden, sharp rise in the yen against the dollar, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively to keep the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from plummetting, he added.    \par \par \uc2  "The market always overshoots, but if that happens very quickly, I think that the Japanese government would again engage in some kind of aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop speculative moves. They've got to do it. Otherwise the dollar would plummet." ($1=108.81 yen).    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040317e03h000bk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
