\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Options: Yen Volatilities Continue To Climb  \b0\par\par\uc2 552 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 19:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Volatilities implied by dollar/yen \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options continued to climb in New York Wednesday as the yen extended its broad-based gains in the spot market.  \par \par \uc2 The rebound in the yen since reaching a five-month low above Y112 against the dollar two weeks ago has accelerated this week amid speculation that Japanese monetary authorities are easing up on aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  tactics.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar slid to Y108 for the first time in a month, but the biggest decliner of the day has been euro/yen, which fell over 1% to a six-week low of Y131.94 before recovering back above Y132.  \par \par \uc2 The speed of the move has surprised market participants, who expected the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to maintain a heavy presence until Japan's March 31 fiscal year-end.  \par \par \uc2 Wednesday afternoon, the dollar was lower at Y108.18 from Y108.78. The euro was trading around $1.2218, down from $1.2260 late Tuesday in New York, and was lower against the yen at Y132.13 from Y133.40.  \par \par \uc2 Demand for options to both bet on, and protect against, further gains in the yen has led to a surge in volatilities over the past couple sessions.  \par \par \uc2 "There was very high demand for yen calls this morning," noted Waiman Leung, vice president of derivatives trading at Credit Suisse Asset Management in New York. "Very short-term vols spiked as specs want to take advantage of a move to the Y105 area."  \par \par \uc2 And it's not just demand for short-dated options that's soaring. "It's a cross the curve," said a trader at a Japanese bank in New York.  \par \par \uc2 One-month at-the-money dollar/yen options implied volatility of 10.60%/10.90%, up from 10.10%/10.60% earlier in Tokyo and 9.90%/10.20% Tuesday in New York.  \par \par \uc2 Implied volatilities for one-week dollar/yen options also continued to climb, to 11.70%/12.30% from 11.25%/12.25% in Tokyo and 11.10%/11.40% in New York on Tuesday, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Volatilities on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options gauge the anticipated moves in the underlying \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with a higher volatility implying a wider trading range.  \par \par \uc2 One-month risk-reversals on dollar/yen favor yen calls at 1.80%/2.20%, above 1.45%/1.75% in Tokyo and 1.10%/1.40% in New York on Tuesday. Risk-reversals measure the market preference for either put or call options by the premium or discount applied.  \par \par \uc2 One-month at-the-money euro/dollar options implied volatility of 11.60%/11.90%, down slightly from 11.70%/12.05% earlier in Tokyo and 11.70%/12.00% in New York on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 However, one-month euro/yen option volatilities continued to rise, to 12.35%/12.65% from 11.55%/11.95% in Tokyo and 11.40%/11.70% in New York Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 One-month risk-reversals on dollar/yen were neutral at -0.2%/0.2%, after euro calls/dollar puts were favored by 0.0%/0.4% the day before.  \par \par \uc2 -By Tom Barkley, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4385; tom.barkley@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 (Jamie McGeever contributed to this report.) [ 17-03-04 1833GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 1077 4127 44427  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040317e03h000uw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
