\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW: Medley: US Job Market Not Worrying Fed - Yet  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Hiroshi Inoue  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 1,094 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 08:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The U.S. Federal Reserve isn't worried about recent weak domestic jobs growth, but may rethink its outlook if the job market stagnates for another three months, says Richard Medley, chairman of Medley Global Advisors.  \par \par \uc2 Right now, the Fed is taking one small step at a time rather than setting a longer-term plan, he said.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't think the Fed's going to jump to any conclusions yet because their models still strongly indicate that job growth will begin," the influential investment adviser told Dow Jones Newswires in a recent interview in New York.  \par \par \uc2 "But I do think that if you got two or three more months of this - say another quarter of jobs growth at these very low levels - then the Fed would have to do one of two things," he added.  \par \par \uc2 Either the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would need to conclude by the time of its next Humphrey Hawkins review in the summer that its employment growth predictions were a bit high, or "it will conclude that there's something going on in the U.S. labor force that makes those jobless figures a lot less important than they used to be in previous recoveries."  \par \par \uc2 The Fed has signaled that jobs growth - or the lack of it - is key to future policy direction, while prices of bonds, stocks and the dollar have moved sharply after each month's employment data.  \par \par \uc2 February figures damped Fed rate-rise speculation as the data showed U.S. non-farm payrolls grew 21,000 last month, much weaker than the 125,000 new jobs economists were expecting.  \par \par \uc2 The Federal Open Market Committee Tuesday, in response, kept official interest rates at 1% and in its accompanying statement said that new hiring had "lagged" - a less upbeat view than it expressed in January.  \par \par \uc2 Medley said his firm is looking into the second option - that a demographic shift in the labor force makes the jobless numbers less important as a predictor of consumption and demand and overall growth in the economy.  \par \par \uc2 The investment advisor's client notes, which are available only by subscription, are closely followed by market participants because of Medley's perceived close ties to policy makers. Medley's previous positions have included managing director and chief policy adviser at Soros Fund Management and Associate Director of Yale University Center for International Finance. In the 1980s he was Chief Economist for the U.S. House of Representatives Committee on Banking Finance and Urban Affairs and for the Senate Democratic Leadership, according to a biography posted on his firm's Web site.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par      Global "Liquidity Bubble" \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While jobs growth is slow now, the Fed is also trying to work out a way to get out of its easy monetary policy when hiring of labor eventually accelerates, but in a way that doesn't rattle financial markets.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Expectations that the Fed will keep rates on hold for some time, plus additional liquidity being pumped into U.S. markets by Asian central banks' \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stop the dollar falling, has pushed long-term rates to very low levels.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There's a huge liquidity bubble in the world right now," Medley said. "The Fed understands...that that's causing distortions in credit markets."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yield on 10-year U.S. Treasury notes Tuesday fell to its lowest level since mid-July last year at 3.66% after the Fed's policy decision to keep rates steady.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Fed, just like the Bank of Japan with its own ultra-easy policy, is wondering "how do we get on the other side of this liquidity bubble with minimum disruption to markets," Medley said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While financial market participants and private-sector economists need to set specific forecasts on U.S. monetary policy, the Fed has "no clear path to an exit" from its easy monetary settings, he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "They take small steps and keep moving forward rather than having grand plans," he added. "They know that no matter how much planning they do, the market reaction will always be different for what they plan for and they don't want to get locked into a rigid plan of steps that may be inappropriate for market conditions."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Medley said there is a range of views among Fed officials about where policy is headed.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "You can find Fed officials who believe they'll be on hold all year," he said. "You can find Fed officials who believe they'll be raising rates in the fourth quarter of this year. You could find people to say just about anything you want to."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fed Chairman Alan Greenspan is the one person that counts in this equation, he said. "And right now he seems to see no reason to make a decision about what you do in the future because there's plenty of time to decide that as the (economic) numbers come in."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par      Lauds BOJ Fukui's Stance \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Turning to Japan, Medley said he believes Greenspan wasn't criticizing the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's recent heavy yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets in his speech March 2.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In that speech, Greenspan focused more on the need for an exit strategy from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's ultra-easy monetary policy rather than making an "explicit commentary on current Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "He used the future tense, which is a lot different to an explicit warning about \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," Medley said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The investment adviser understands why Japanese officials are being cautious in assessing Japan's economic recovery.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Japanese officials, particularly at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , can be forgiven for being the last people in the world for wanting to believe that this is real," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Medley lauded BOJ Governor Toshihiko Fukui's "aggressive" policy stance, which has "paid off."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Our view is very strongly that from the policy perspective, they're doing the right thing and they should continue to do it," he added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Medley also played down the possible impact on Japan of slower growth in the U.S.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Japan's continued recovery has less to do with the United States than it ever has in the postwar period," he said. "The intra-Asian economic engine is perfectly capable of ensuring Japan grows toward recovery and on a recovery track even if the U.S. grows at 2% or 3%."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Hiroshi Inoue, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2995; Hiroshi.Inoue@dowjones.com (Iain McDonald contributed to this article.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Hugh Lawson [ 17-03-04 0702GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040317e03h0007t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
