\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Interest Rate Hike On Hold  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,319 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 07:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US SUMMARY: Focus On Economics, Earnings \par  \par DJIA          10184.67  gain 81.78      up 0.8% \par NASDAQ        1943.09   gain 3.89       up 0.2% \par S&P 500       1110.70   gain 6.21       up 0.6% \par Dow Future    10155.00  loss 7.00       dn 0.1% \par NASDAQ Future 1407.50   gain 2.00       up 0.1% \par S&P Future    1111.10   loss 0.20          0.0% \par 10-Yr US Treasury: 3.68% down 0.09 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Wall Street looked past an underwhelming statement by the Federal Reserve on Tuesday and rebounded smartly as bargain hunters picked up stocks battered by a week of heavy losses. Buyers of Treasurys took heart in the Fed's implication for a long lull in any interest-rate hikes.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 STOCKS: The market focused on economics and earnings, particularly a bright outlook from manufacturing giant 3M Co., which upped its quarterly and full-year estimates. The Commerce Department also reported that the construction sector remained healthy.  \par \par \uc2 Bargain hunters, keeping the upbeat economic and earnings data in mind, restored the market's momentum late in the session.  \par \par \uc2 The Fed said it could be "patient" in adjusting rates, the same language it used in February. But with investors worried of late about the nation's job growth, the Fed's statement did nothing to assuage Wall Street's fears.  \par \par \uc2 Without new jobs, the economic recovery could stall as companies try to wring more productivity out of their current \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  instead of expanding.  \par \par \uc2 "It's a bit like a game of chicken," said Chris Wolfe, global head of equities for J.P. Morgan Private Bank. "Productivity will drive the economy for now, but soon investors will have to look at whether companies are making the most of their capital. And the companies are facing higher labor costs and having to decide if that's where they want to put their money."  \par \par \uc2 "I think the market's rebound is responding to the data we've received," said Stuart Freeman, chief equity strategist for A.G. Edwards & Sons. "The bombings in Spain have taken up a lot of attention, but I think people are now starting to look at the economic data, which on balance has been positive."  \par \par \uc2 Mergers and acquisitions continued in the financial sector, one of the few stalwarts in recent weeks. BancWest Corp., a Hawaii-based subsidiary of France's BNP Paribas, said it would acquire Community First Bankshares Inc. for about $1.2 billion in cash.  \par \par \uc2 BONDS: Indications that the Federal Reserve will remain very patient before raising interest rates triggered a strong afternoon rally in Treasurys.  \par \par \uc2 The Federal Open Market Committee, the Fed's rate-setting arm, kept official interest rates at a more-than-four-decade low of 1%. The FOMC said the odds of an "unwelcome" fall in inflation are "almost equal" to the risk of inflation.  \par \par \uc2 The Fed also noted that new hiring has "lagged" - a less upbeat view on labor markets than it expressed in late January.  \par \par \uc2 The statement turned around the bond market, which had traded lower in price prior to the FOMC decision, and pushed yields down to near eight-month lows. Traders had worried that the Fed would adopt a neutral stance on inflation risks and perhaps pave the way for higher rates later this year.  \par \par \uc2 "It's a major statement of where the Fed is leaning... Rates are really on hold at least through the end of this year, if not well into the beginning of next year," said Mary Ann Hurley, vice president of fixed-income trading at D.A. Davidson in Seattle.  \par \par \uc2 Craig Coats, head of fixed income at Keefe, Bruyette & Woods in New York expects the exuberance in Treasurys to be short-lived. "I think we've probably seen the bulk of the rally, and we'll be here for the next couple of weeks until we get the next payroll number," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The next Labor Department report on monthly non-farm payrolls growth is slated for April 2.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ASIAN SUMMARY: Stocks Show Strength On Fed's Easy Stance \par  \par USD-Yen             108.60       loss  0.18    dn 0.2% \par AUD-USD             0.7426       gain  0.0006  up 0.09% \par Nikkei 225          11384.92     gain  142.63  up 1.3% \par Hang Seng           12974.67     gain  42.05   up 0.3% \par JGB Yield           1.2950 up 0.0100 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Tokyo stocks advanced Wednesday after Wall Street's gains spurred broad buying. The dollar was down against the Japanese yen. Prices of Japanese government bonds lost slightly.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 STOCKS: Taking heart from the Fed's generous policy stance, investors picked up cheap shares on the hopes that easy U.S. monetary conditions will help stimulate demand for Japanese products.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BONDS: Prices of JGB's gave up some ground to advancing shares.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : The dollar's slide against the yen also pushed down European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against both the yen and the dollar. Many players are taking out hedging strategies to cope with a lower dollar if the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fails to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stop its fall.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, China is easing restrictions on foreign investment in the country's booming retail sector, aiming for full compliance with market-opening commitments made to the World Trade Organization, state media reports said Wednesday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK: Stocks May Try For Mild Gains \par  \par Euro-USD            1.2278      gain  0.0004  up 0.02% \par Stlg-USD            1.8154      gain  0.0028  up 0.1% \par USD-Franc           1.2785      loss  0.0004  dn 0.1% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ZURICH (Dow Jones)--European stock markets are expected to open steady. But government bonds offer some real competition on the prospect that low Fed interest rates will last for the forseeable future, and that the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will offer up the same.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 STOCKS: European shares are called steady at the open, with U.K. spreadbettor IG Index expecting the FTSE down 8 points to 4420, the Dax flat at 3822, and the CAC down 2 at 3586.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European stocks moved cautiously higher Tuesday as investors snapped up cheap shares following recent losses, but nagging doubts about the pace of global recovery kept a lid on the enthusiasm.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A survey Tuesday showed financial analysts are more skeptical about Germany's growth prospects in March than February. The German ZEW March business survey came in at 57.6, well below analysts' forecasts of 65.0 and February's 69.9 reading.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CSFB strategists said that with stocks breaking through support levels and concerns about terrorism on the rise, the next few weeks are likely to prove difficult for equity markets.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BONDS: Euro-zone government bond prices are likely to imitate the gains in Treasurys and open on a firm note.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hopes that the ECB could actually lower interest rates should also support the market, on the theory that the Madrid bombings may need some monetary counterweight to keep the economy in balance.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bunds and gilts drifted lower Tuesday on caution ahead of the Federal Reserve's monetary policy verdict.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There's been some precautionary paring of long positions, with stronger equities adding a little impetus to the down-move in bonds," a dealer with a French bank said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While keeping an eye out for the Bank of England's latest minutes, investors may take solace in the Fed's relaxed position and reaquire bonds they sold on Tuesday.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : The dollar hit a three-week low against the yen Tuesday, but was little changed against the euro after Federal Reserve policymakers signaled no shift in the outlook for interest rates.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen gained amid continued speculation that the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would ease up on large-scale \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The dollar fell as far as Y108.65, a level not seen since late February. The dollar's slide against the yen started a chain reaction during the New York session, pushing European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  down against both the yen and the dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CALENDAR: Wednesday, March 17: German, U.S. Prices \par GMT \par 0700  GER  Feb     Producer Price Index \par 0745  FRA  Jan     Current Account \par 0930  UK   Mar     Bank of England meeting minutes \par 0930  UK   Feb     Unemployment \par 1200  US   Mar 13  MBA Refinancing Index \par 1230  UK           Chancellor of the Exchequer's Budget \par 1330  US   Feb     CPI \par 1630  US           Fed Chmn Greenspan speaks via satellite \par 2350  JPN          Weekly Portfolio Flows Data   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; dennis.baker@dowjones.com [ 17-03-04 0600GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Vivendi Universal (V): FY Preliminary Earnings \par Consensus net loss (JCF, 29 analys