\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Sinks Against Yen, Market Shrugs Off Fed  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Tom Barkley  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 849 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 22:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK -- The dollar hit a three-week low against the yen Tuesday, but was little changed against the euro after Federal Reserve policymakers signaled no shift in the outlook for interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 The Federal Open Market Committee not only left its key interest rate at a 46-year low of 1%, as expected, but also reiterated its stance that inflationary pressures are low enough that it "can be patient in removing its policy accommodation."  \par \par \uc2 Fed officials also acknowledged the recent batch of disappointing employment reports, noting in their post-meeting statement that, "Although job losses have slowed, new hiring has lagged."  \par \par \uc2 The statement reaffirmed market expectations that the Fed would hold off from raising rates until at least late 2004, which may continue to weigh on the dollar. Low U.S. rates have been a major factor in the dollar's long-term downtrend because it makes it more difficult to attract enough foreign investment to fund the U.S. current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 However, the dollar was mostly directionless in choppy trading against most \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the Fed's decision.  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar consequences will be marginal at best," said Jason Bonanca, director and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Credit Suisse First Boston in New York. "The Fed's still not talking about removing policy accommodation, (so) this is not dovish enough to reignite dollar bearishness."  \par \par \uc2 Marc Chandler, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC Bank in New York, agreed that the statement included "nothing really new substantially to elicit much of a response in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market."  \par \par \uc2 Instead, the biggest moves in the dollar came against the yen amid continued speculation that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would ease up on large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The dollar fell as far as 108.65 yen, a level not seen since late February. The dollar's slide against the yen started a chain reaction during the New York session, pushing European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  down against both the yen and the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 That, in turn, helped the dollar recoup most of its overnight losses against the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which were due in large part to continued jitters about an increase in global terrorism following last week's deadly bombings in Madrid.  \par \par \uc2 Late Tuesday afternoon, the dollar was trading around 108.78 yen, down sharply from 110.30 yen late Monday in New York. The euro was at $1.2260, down slightly from $1.2272, and was lower against the yen at 133.40 yen from 135.40 yen. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at 1.2778 francs, up from 1.2751 francs, while sterling remained higher on the day at $1.8114 versus $1.8055 late Monday.  \par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , had apparently switched tactics by buying dollars even when the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was rallying. The more aggressive stance was viewed in the market as a move to push down the yen ahead of the March 31 fiscal year end to assist Japanese exporters and others who need to repatriate money from abroad.  \par \par \uc2 However, official support for the dollar has become more erratic in the last week, with agent banks of the BOJ apparently pulling bids and sometimes buying up dollars again after a sharp drop.  \par \par \uc2 While investors were expecting that Japanese monetary authorities would ease up on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the fiscal year end on March 31, an article in Tuesday's edition of the Nikkei Financial Daily cited \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials as predicting an end to large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by month end.  \par \par \uc2 While many analysts dismissed the article as both speculation and stating the obvious - that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can't continue at the current record-breaking pace - the news has emboldened short-term players to push the dollar down and test BOJ resolve.  \par \par \uc2 "I think it's a speculative punt to a big degree, to bet that dollar/yen is going to go down," said Scott Ainsbury, portfolio manager at \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Concenpts, a hedge fund based in New York. He noted that short-dated option volatilities jumped to their highest levels in six weeks as speculators bought derivatives to bet on further declines in the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Overnight, the BOJ's decision to keep monetary policy unchanged, as expected, didn't have much effect on the market. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also reiterated in its monthly assessment that Japan's economy is "recovering gradually."  \par \par \uc2 Japanese officials also defended the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, with Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki saying that the "stance remains unchanged" and that "\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates should move stably in reflection of (economic) fundamentals."  \par \par \uc2 Still, against a backdrop of improving Japanese economic conditions, less extreme market positioning and good demand for Japanese assets, Japanese monetary authorities are expected to become "a bit more relaxed" about the yen's exchange rate with the dollar, said Rebecca Patterson, global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan in New York.  \par \par \uc2 -By Tom Barkley, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4385  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     tom.barkley@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Jamie McGeever contributed to this story.) [ 03-16-04 1659ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020040316e03g0000w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
