\par\par \b \uc2 2nd UPDATE: Japan Keeps Selling Yen, BOJ Policy On Hold  \b0\par\par\uc2 840 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 10:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Updates to add BOJ policy decision, comments from BOJ Governor Fukui.)  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par      By Miyako Takebe \par      Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japanese policy makers held firm Tuesday in their resolve to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets amid growing speculation that they are looking for a way to end their massive yen-selling campaign.  \par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said Japan's policy on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't changed, and it will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  whenever the yen moves out of line with economic fundamentals.  \par \par \uc2 "I won't comment on daily \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels, but our stance remains unchanged," Sadakazu Tanigaki told reporters. "We believe \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates should move stably in reflection of (economic) fundamentals."  \par \par \uc2 His comments follow a plunge Monday in the dollar against the yen to a two-week low of Y109.25 after the Nikkei Financial Daily reported "some \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials" in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  are expecting "large-scale" yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ease after the end of this month.  \par \par \uc2 But Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui indicated that there are no such concerns at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that the BOJ supports the recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  sanctioned by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is responsible for Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.  \par \par \uc2 Fukui also implied that the U.S. Federal Reserve understands why \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily, playing down speculation that the U.S. is unhappy with Japan's recent bouts of yen selling.  \par \par \uc2 While Japan's economy is in deflation, it is vulnerable to external shocks, such as \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market volatility, Fukui said.  \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't contradict monetary policy. (Fed) Chairman (Alan) Greenspan understands this well," he said at a news conference.  \par \par \uc2 When Japan overcomes deflation there may come a time when less aggressive monetary easing and less aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be needed, Fukui said.  \par \par \uc2 "But now isn't that time," he said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par      BOJ Committed To Ultra-Easy Policy \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The BOJ's board voted unanimously Tuesday to hold steady its ultra-easy monetary policy settings, maintaining its liquidity target in a range of Y30 trillion to Y35 trillion.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With domestic financial markets calm ahead of Japan's fiscal year-end on March 31 and strong economic data recently, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's board decided there was no immediate need to boost the amount of cash flowing through Japan's financial system.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 And in its latest economic report, the BOJ left its core economic assessment unchanged, repeating for the fourth straight month that the economy is "recovering gradually."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "As for the outlook, Japan's economy is anticipated to continue recovering moderately," the BOJ said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  upgraded its assessment of several key segments of the economy: business investment, consumer spending and corporate goods prices.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Domestic corporate goods prices have been rising," mainly due to higher commodities prices, the BOJ said, adding corporate goods prices "are expected to continue increasing for some time."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But Fukui said recent increases in basic material prices won't immediately flow through to the core consumer price index, which the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  uses as a guide to setting policy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We firmly voted to keep the current monetary easing until we clear three conditions," he said. Those conditions are that on-year changes in the consumer price index must rise above zero, remain above zero and the majority of board members mustn't expect deflation to return.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Given that the BOJ doesn't expect deflation to loosen its grip on the economy anytime soon and the Finance Ministry remains committed to its policy of protecting the economy by keeping the yen from appreciating, it isn't surprising that Japan continued to make its presence felt in Tokyo markets Tuesday with some traders saying the BOJ had placed covert orders to buy dollars.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Nikkei report comes in the wake of unprecedented \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by Japanese monetary authorities, which sold Y10 trillion in the first two months of 2004 after selling a record Y20 trillion in 2003  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tanigaki said the MOF "will continue to take appropriate action in the event there are excessive moves."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In addition, traders and strategists played down the Nikkei report, saying they don't expect the Finance Ministry to stop \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  yet. They said \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  remains keen to discourage speculators from taking big positions in the market such as the amassing of long-yen positions last month that helped push the dollar to a three-year low of Y105.16.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The Japanese authorities' intention is to get rid of speculators," said Mitsuo Imaizumi, deputy general manager of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at Daiwa Securities SMBC.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Others said that the report only noted dissatisfaction among officials at the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The MOF is ultimately responsible for yen \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and hence the market will place particular importance on comments by MOF officials for any signs that they might relax on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," UBS added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; miyako.takebe@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Hiroshi Inoue, Jonathan Soble, Michiyo Seki and Takashi Nakamichi contributed to this article.) [ 16-03-04 0937GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040316e03g000di\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
