\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Dlr Fall On BOJ Speculation May Be Overdone  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,027 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 21:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Jamie McGeever and Tom Barkley \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders betting \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will soon scale back its heavy \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets had best be careful.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 A report Monday from The Nikkei Financial Daily said "some \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials" in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  expect "large-scale" yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ease after the end of this month. A day after the report's publication, the dollar fell to a three-week low of Y108.65 as emboldened investors test the resolve of the same Japanese monetary authorities whose \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped drive the dollar to a five-month high last week over Y112.00.  \par \par \uc2 But this immediate reaction aside, many market participants are skeptical. Some doubt the veracity of the Nikkei story, pointing out it was merely an opinion piece, poorly sourced and had no fundamental basis.  \par \par \uc2 The last word on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  tactics - especially where, when and how it decides to ease up on its dollar purchases - remains as unknown as ever. It would be hasty, therefore, to take off all bets against the dollar on the back of one media report.  \par \par \uc2 "Where does the BOJ and MOF draw the line? They won't draw the line at the end of March," said a dealer at a Japanese bank in New York, who said the article was "100% bogus." Only when the U.S. economy starts creating jobs and the Fed responds by tightening monetary policy will the dollar likely pull itself out of its multi-year downtrend. Only then will \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  draw an end to its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said.  \par \par \uc2 In a sense, the report is 100% correct: nobody doubts that the record yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Japanese monetary authorities - at least Y30 trillion in the last 14 months - is unsustainable and must, at some point, be reduced.  \par \par \uc2 Domestic opposition to spending such huge amounts on building up international reserves instead of achieving more tangible benefits like boosting jobs or economic growth would likely add to growing international displeasure. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan and U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow have both voiced concern in recent weeks that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unsustainable and incompatible with free markets.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Speculators Burned \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on behalf of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , dramatically ramped up dollar purchases from huge levels anyway around a month ago when the dollar was languishing at three and a half-year lows against the yen just above Y105.00. This drove the dollar up to Y112.33 last week.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The finance ministry sells yen in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to ensure Japan's exporters receive as competitive an exchange rate as possible. And with Japan's fiscal year-end on March 31 looming, many investors and observers expected authorities to make sure these exporters got as good a high a rate as possible for balance sheet purposes.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the ministry has pulled its bid in recent sessions, burning a lot of speculators who had banked on official Japanese support for the dollar holding firm. This cleared out a lot of speculative positions, created uncertainty in the market and a reluctance to put on large positions, and raised questions on the MOF's tactics, which authorities no doubt welcomed on all accounts.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Given the ministry's element for surprise, therefore, Rebecca Patterson, global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at JP Morgan in New York, is cautious on reading too much into the Nikkei report.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "They won't let dollar/yen free-fall," Patterson said. Instead, the MOF could well opt to smooth out the dollar's moves against the yen as opposed to specifically target a stronger dollar. This would appear to be the most likely option bearing in mind the apparent easing up of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the last week or so has coincided with the dollar losing some of its general upward momentum.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the three weeks starting around mid-February, when dealers reported a dramatic rise in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  efforts, the dollar was appreciating across the board, Patterson pointed out. This meant Japanese monetary authorities "got more juice" from their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . But in the last week or so, following yet another dismal U.S. employment report, the dollar's corrective rebound has stalled. And so has \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Japanese Officials Sanguine \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Scott Ainsbury, portfolio manager at \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Concepts, a hedge fund based in New York, said he doesn't put much weight in the Nikkei story.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's hard to believe they would spend all that money and pull the bid so close to the finish line," said Ainsbury, referring to March 31. "I think they're just toying with the market a little bit, and they'll pull the bid and suck them in, then squeeze them out of the market one last time."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Predictably enough, perhaps, Japanese officials dismissed the report. "I won't comment on daily \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  levels, but our stance remains unchanged," Sadakazu Tanigaki told reporters. "We believe \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates should move stably in reflection of (economic) fundamentals."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 And BOJ Governor Toshihiko Fukui indicated that there is no cause for concern at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  doesn't contradict monetary policy. Chairman Greenspan understands this well," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the lackluster nature of these official comments suggest the story may indeed carry some weight, said a dealer at another Japanese bank in New York.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I expected more of a denial, more of a defense (of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy)," he said. "And I at least expected them to hold the (Y110) line until the year-end."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But given Japanese monetary authorities' ability to shift tactics and catch the market off-guard, they just might. (Jamie McGeever, who covers \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets in New York, has also reported on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Dow Jones Newswires in Rio de Janeiro and London. Tom Barkley is a special writer covering \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in New York. He previously covered convertible and high-yield debt markets.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -Jamie McGeever; Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Tom Barkley, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4385 tom.barkley@dowjones.com [ 03-16-04 1514ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040316e03g0003m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
