\par\par \b \uc2 MONUMENT SECURITIES: Constraints On Japan  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Policy  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,111 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 14:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Stephen Lewis \par    Of Monument Securities \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--It is hardly news that there are officials within the Bank of Japan (BoJ) who are deeply unhappy with the Japanese authorities' seemingly unlimited readiness to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to hold down the yen. Yesterday's Nikkei story that the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is mulling ways to scale back its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 31 March seems to have been based largely on such sources. Why this faction is opposed to the BoJ's amassing dollars is not entirely clear. Arguably, the policy facilitates the BoJ aim of boosting domestic liquidity. There has been a build-up in yen deposits in the banking system, stemming from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , that has not been wholly sterilised through BoJ money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The BoJ has implemented successive increases in the target for commercial banks' current accounts with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; these moves have kept the monetary base on a sharply rising trajectory, with 16.2% growth recorded in the year to February. Even so, some BoJ officials' doubts over the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy persist. It may be they are grounded in a fear of large numbers. Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves had risen from $496bn at the end of 2002 to $777bn by end-February this year. At that rate, Japan appears soon to be heading for the first trillion-dollar pile of reserves in world history. When many industrial countries get by with reserves that are 10% or less of Japan's holdings, it is perhaps not surprising that some Japanese policymakers are wondering whether there might not be a better way of managing affairs. In the end, though, the decision on Japanese exchange rate policy falls to the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MoF), not to the BoJ. Mr Fukui, the BoJ Governor, made this clear in his press conference today. Mr Tanigaki, the minister responsible, has stated there is no change in Japan's policy of seeking a yen rate that reflects economic fundamentals. The implication is that MoF will continue to favour \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the rate straying too far from the level against the US dollar that it regards as justified by fundamentals. MoF, indeed, has been more aggressive, or at least tactically astute, in pursuing its goal than seemed likely in February when the yen was pressing up against the 105 rate against the US dollar. Then, it seemed, MoF might be satisfied if only it could bring the yen a little way down from that ceiling. However, when a turn in market sentiment allowed some relief from yen strength, the Japanese authorities stepped up their dollar buying in aggressive action that seemed designed to drive the yen lower. It would be strange if, after that impressive demonstration of their resolve to keep the yen competitive, Japanese policymakers were to withdraw from the fray the next time the yen appreciates on the foreign exchanges. Possibly, they expect the markets to have been so cowed by the scale of the BoJ's recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , that the yen will not raise its head again. Mr Takenaka, the Economics Minister, hinted today that may be the case. The problem is that Japan's economy is performing rather too well for international investors to ignore it for long. When there is increasing concern about the durability of the economic upswing in the USA and the euro zone recovery seems more like a mirage, Japan may well seem an attractive home for capital. Its companies may be seen as being in a relatively favourable position to tap in to growth in the wider East Asian economy. Consequently, the yen could regain strength in the months ahead, leaving MoF with a choice between resuming \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or countenancing some loss of Japanese manufacturers' competitiveness. If the global economic background turns out to be less buoyant than seemed likely in the first two months of the year, the pressure on the Japanese authorities to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold down the yen will be no less in future than it has been in recent times. Even so, the necessary scale of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be less, going forward. This is because there was probably a large speculative element in the market's dollar sales in January and February which may not recur. Dollar trading has not turned out to be the one-way street that many speculators expected. The best guess must be that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue in the months ahead but will fall short of matching its scale so far this year. There is no need to posit a shift in Japanese policy to look for a reduced flow of BoJ-held dollars into the US bond markets. So far this year, Fed figures show, foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings of US bonds have risen by $93bn. Mr Greenspan argues this amount is small in relation to the size of the US bond market; he concludes that there would not be much market impact if foreign central banks were to stop increasing their holdings. However, any conceivable change in the flow of funds pattern would be small relative to the size of the US bond market. What is important is how much the shift would affect yields at the margin. Given the dynamics involved in the US bond market, even a small shift in the funds inflow could set off a sharp rise in yields. As it is, the US Mortgage Bankers Association (MBA) is projecting a fall in mortgage refinancing from $2.5bn last year to $1.1bn this year. A significant rise in long yields would hurt purchase mortgages, which MBA forecasts to hold up this year close to last year's $1.3bn rate, and business investment.  \par \par \uc2 -By Stephen Lewis: 44 20 7338 0179: analysismonumentsecurities.com  \par \par \uc2 (Stephen Lewis is chief economist at Monument Securities Ltd., London, independent brokers specializing in institutional business.)  \par \par \uc2 Opinions expressed are those of the author, and not of Dow Jones Newswires.  \par \par \uc2 This column is published for information only, and it neither constitutes, nor is to be construed as, an offer to buy or sell investments. The information and opinions expressed herein are based on sources the author believes to be reliable, but he cannot represent that they are accurate or complete. Any information herein is given in good faith, but is subject to change without notice. No liability is accepted whatsoever by Monument Securities Ltd., employees and associated companies for any direct or consequential loss arising from this article. Monument Securities Ltd. is regulated by the SFA and is a member of the London Stock Exchange, LIFFE and ISMA. [ 03-16-04 0803ET ]  \par \par \uc2 1089  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040316e03g0001b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
