\par\par \b \uc2 US Treasuries ease on  \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention \highlight\uc2  report.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Mariko Hayashibara  \par \uc2 578 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 05:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 16 (Reuters) - U.S. Treasury prices eased slightly on Tuesday as Asian investors worried about the possible fallout if the Japanese authorities slowed their massive buying of dollars in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 Such speculation grew after the Nihon Keizai financial daily reported on Tuesday that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was considering ending its large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after March, when the business year ends.  \par \par \uc2 "As the BOJ's purchases are believed to be providing big support to the Treasury market, such speculation is hurting private investors' appetite," said Kiichi Murashima, market and economic analysis director at Nikko Citigroup in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 But declines were limited as investors waited for the outcome of a Federal Reserve policy meeting later in the day. Safe-haven buying amid rising security risks after last week's deadly bombings in Spain lent some support.  \par \par \uc2 Benchmark 10-year notes were yielding 3.77 percent, up slightly from 3.76 percent in late New York on Monday after touching an eight-month low of 3.68 percent last week.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The 30-year bond  was unchanged, yielding 4.71 percent. Five-year notes\par            were also unchanged at 2.73 percent.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At the very short end, yields on two-year notes were 1.54 percent versus 1.53 percent.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tokyo funnels most of the dollars it buys through \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  into U.S. Treasuries. Reduced \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would mean less appetite for U.S. government paper as a place to stash \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  proceeds, which has been cited as a key factor keeping U.S. yields low in the past year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But some analysts ponder whether the influence of reduced Japanese buying of Treasuries would be as serious as the market expects.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 According to JP Morgan Chase in Tokyo, Treasury purchases by Asian central banks are depressing yields by 30 basis points below their fair value at the short end of the curve.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Since most of the Treasuries Japan holds are in short-term paper, the actual impact to the whole market may be much smaller, as (Federal Reserve Chairman Alan) Greenspan argues," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Greenspan said earlier this month that the effect of a reduction in Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on U.S. financial markets would be small.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The reason is that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves are heavily concentrated in short-term maturities; moreover, the overall market in short-term dollar assets, combining both public and private instruments, is huge relative to the size of asset holdings of Asian monetary authorities," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FED WORDING EYED  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Most investors expect the Fed to hold interest rates steady, but some are bracing for a possible change of wording in its statement.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Financial markets have pushed back expectations of a rate rise until later this year or early 2005 after the latest in a long line of weak payroll reports.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With growth in non-farm payrolls almost non-existent, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is expected to repeat its promise that it can remain "patient" before starting to raise the federal funds rate from 1.0 percent, its lowest level since 1958.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A statement from the Fed is expected after Tuesday's policy meeting, around 2:15 p.m. eastern time (1915 GMT).  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors are also waiting to see U.S. housing starts, which are due at 1330 GMT. Economists polled by Reuters estimated that starts rose to a seasonally adjusted annualised rate of 1.930 million in February from 1.903 million in January.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040316e03g000ji\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
