\par\par \b \uc2 OFF THE RUN: Foreign Central Banks Do Matter For Bonds  \b0\par\par\uc2 895 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 21:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Agnes T. Crane \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan may downplay the importance of Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  purchases of Treasurys on U.S. capital markets, but as Monday's choppy market trade showed, the psychological impact they have is hard to ignore.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 The Nikkei Financial Daily reported in its Tuesday edition that the Bank of Japan, which acts in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on behalf of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is considering a substantial slowing of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  after its fiscal year ends on March 31. The report hit the news wires only hours after Treasury data strongly suggested that such \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  led to a record month of foreign purchases of U.S. government securities in January. The implications were not lost on the market.  \par \par \uc2 The benchmark 10-year note yield shot up three basis points while the two-year note also rose roughly three basis points in the immediate aftermath of the news report.  \par \par \uc2 "If for nothing else than a psychological impact on the market" the Bank of Japan and its appetite for U.S. government securities matters, said Thomas Girard, senior portfolio manager at Weiss, Peck & Greer in New York, who manages approximately $6.5 billion in fixed income investments.  \par \par \uc2 Although the selloff proved short-lived, the knee-jerk reaction to the Nikkei report indicates broader-based weakness could weigh on prices further should the Japanese government actually follow through, simply because of its dominating presence in the Treasury market.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan has become more aggressive in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulations in recent months as it attempts to bolster the dollar against the yen. A weaker Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  makes the nation's goods more competitive in foreign markets, while, in theory, it also combat deflation by stimulating inflationary pressures.  \par \par \uc2 In January, the Japanese monetary authorities vacuumed up a record $67 billion worth of U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as it fought to keep the dollar from falling below Y105. A sizable portion of these dollars was recycled into the U.S. Treasury market, helping to push foreign central purchases to a record level.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Keeping Interest Rates Low \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 According to the latest data from the Treasury Department, released earlier Monday, foreign official buying of Treasurys reached $26.9 billion in January, a new record that helped pushed total foreign purchases of government bonds to an all-time high of $46.9 billion. These same institutions bought $4.4 billion in agencies, the debt issued by government sponsored enterprises such as Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac, the two largest sources of home financing.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Alex Li, interest rate strategist at Credit Suisse First Boston in New York, said foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  purchases do "have an impact on Treasury yields and particularly in the front end." His firm estimates that the two-year note would be 30 to 35 basis points higher without the stepped-up foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  investment in Treasurys over the last 18 months.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Treasury Department doesn't detail how much each foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  buys in a given month, but analysts say the numbers square with data released from Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , indicating that Bank of Japan was behind the bulk of the purchases.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve custody data, which record total holdings of foreign central banks, showed an increase of approximately $32 billion in Treasurys in January while the Treasury data reported a $31.9 billion increase in Japan's Treasury investments in the same month. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , meanwhile, reported a $30.4 billion increase in its foreign securities, mostly Treasurys, for January.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Japan is definitely recirculating their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollars," said Gerald Lucas, head of U.S. Treasury and agency strategy at Banc of America Securities in New York. He doesn't believe the Japanese are going to pull the plug after the fiscal year end.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As for the rest of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  proceeds in January, Lucas noted that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  increased its bank deposits with Japanese and foreign banks operating in Japan by $38 billion during the same period.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Still A Ways To Go \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The selling of Treasurys that erupted after the Nikkei report lacked follow-through and Treasurys prices reversed course in afternoon trade as domestic investors saw the cheaper prices as a buying opportunity, especially given the sluggish pace of job creation and a Federal Reserve reluctant to raise interest rates.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This news report clearly presents some risk to Treasurys...but overall investors are focusing on the bigger picture," said Li of Credit Suisse First Boston.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The fact that the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  apparently \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to buy dollars just minutes after the Nikkei report also gave investors pause as to whether the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is seriously contemplating a more laissez-faire \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's sincerely doubtful that they are going to pull the plug after Mar. 31," said Lucas. He reckons Japan first must eradicate deflation before it abandons an activist stance in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Plus, its up to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 " not the Bank of Japan to decide what policies to pursue, Lucas added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Agnes T. Crane covers has covered U.S. credit markets for over three years.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Agnes T. Crane, Dow Jones Newswires, 201-938-2122; \par agnes.crane@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 15-03-04 2041GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040315e03f000wl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
