\par\par \b \uc2 FXAnalytics Focus: Dollar/Yen: Beware Of The Ides Of MoF  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,313 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 22:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By David Gilmore \par    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Analytics \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Is Japan on the verge of pulling the plug on its massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy (obsession)? Based on a wire headline today drawn from the Nihon Keizai Shimbun article, the answer is a strong maybe. But as is often the case with eye-catching headlines, the reality was far short of the news-breaking snap. Nevertheless it did highlight a growing rift within the BoJ and between the BoJ Governor Fukui and his MoF \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy architects on the one hand and foreign central bankers, including Greenspan, and dissident BoJ board members and staffers, on the other.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 And regardless of the real intent of the report the headline sparked a slide in dollar/yen under 110 to 109.25 and then a move back up to 110.50, essentially unchanged as the text of the Nikkei story emerged.  \par \par \uc2 Addressing the context of the report first, it is important to note \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy in every respect is controlled by MoF and not the BoJ. At the end of the day if the BoJ wants to shift monetary policy away from an emphasis on exchange rates (zero-interest-rate bound) to an emphasis on interest rates, MoF is under no obligation to change any part of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies. On the other hand, unless the BoJ can convince MoF to reduce its dependence on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the BoJ will have very little control over monetary policy (money supply) if massive amounts of yen are being sold by the government. So any BoJ-led discussion of trimming massive yen selling \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  conceived and ordered by MoF is really about taking back control over monetary policy as improving economic conditions warrant a return to normal interest rates (something greater than zero) and the return of a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  with a mandate to check inflation expectations (distorted JGB market from years of a liquidity trap pose serious upside risks to market rates in a sustainable recovering economy.  \par \par \uc2 More specifically the headline missed another key piece of the context of the Nikkei story. The Nikkei report ran as a column (BoJ Watch)...an opinion of the author and not part of general news, though it referenced concerns of BoJ and foreign central banks over the sustainability of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. Anyone who followed Greenspan's remarks to the Economics Club of New York earlier this month knows the Fed Chairman very publicly raised doubt about the sustainability of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. Greenspan even went so far to suggest its demise is imminent (as Japan's economy recovers).  \par \par \uc2 And then there is the issue of growing restlessness by the Bush administration over Japan's unprecedented meddling in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. U.S. Treasury Secretary Snow made this abundantly clear when he stressed markets not governments are the best arbiters of prices (implying \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels). And he explicitly said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be kept to a minimum. All this was said after MoF's shocking \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the March 5 U.S. February jobs report.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is quickly entering the U.S. presidential debate. Kerry's key international economic policy adviser Prestowitz told a Japanese reporter recently that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a export subsidy and Kerry if elected would press Japan to end it. If Prestowitz is anything to go by on Kerry and the dollar, Kerry is no Rubin dollar Democrat. He sounds more like a Reagan dollar Republican...weaker is better.  \par \par \uc2 But as I insinuated earlier, the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate at the BoJ has Governor Fukui on the side of MoF...no need to exit large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  anytime soon. Fukui is by far the most MoF-compliant central banker since the BoJ was granted "full" independence. Hayami, Fukui's predecessor, would have had little tolerance for the antics of the likes of MoF's Mizoguchi, who is a more devout practitioner of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  than Sakakibara and Kuroda (last two MoF International Bureau Chiefs) could ever dream of being. No doubt there would have been internecine warfare between MoF and the BoJ over \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if Hayami were at the helm of the BoJ. Markets may be looking the other way due to the ongoing problem of deflation in Japan. But if inflation indeed resurfaces, the BoJ could find itself lacking market credibility in a hurry.  \par \par \uc2 Finally there is lots of chatter in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  land among those who follow MoF and the BoJ closely that MoF has made it clear to traders it will not tolerate dollar/yen under 110 into fiscal-year-end (end of the month). This message was allegedly given to buy and sell side traders who are part of MoF's inner circle of market contacts. And allegedly MoF has indicated that it anticipates having to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  less starting in April (the new fiscal year) as private investors (pension trusts, Kampo, and life insurance companies) in Japan are expected to increase their purchases of foreign assets. Will private capital outflows reduce the need for official outflows in April? I am skeptical. Typically Japanese institutional investors take weeks to months in shaping and acting on international portfolio allocations. Firms must reach consensus (and in the note too distant past sectors also needed to reach consensus) before major new allocations are made. If MoF's unprecedented purchases of dollars and US Treasuries is anything to go by, we doubt Japan's private investors will be in a rush to get long dollars and Treasuries when U.S. rates are at four decade lows and the dollar in a two-year downtrend. It is also worth noting that market chatter immediately after MoF's March 5 massacre (now put as high as $20bln in total \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) noted MoF was letting its market contacts know it wanted dollar/yen above 115 by fiscal-year-end. But that predated a very likely US protest of MoF's March 05 \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  slaughter.  \par \par \uc2 Bottom line. I think MoF is committed to keeping 110 floor into fiscal year end (not really sure why as mark-to-market rules are usually liberally applied). It may be an Everest issue...because it is there. And it is reasonable to look for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  amounts to diminish in April and the rest of the year as it is unsustainable...distorts the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and US Treasury market (and US credit markets generally), cedes monetary policy from the BoJ to MoF and creates significant distortions in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's domestic interest rate markets. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also unsustainable because \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy is in all likelihood emerging from deflation and a normalization of monetary policy is a must and the sooner the better. Massive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is incompatible with a normalization in monetary policy.  \par \par \uc2 Lastly, I think there is a rogue element at work inside Japan's international bureau at MoF. Yes, an official Nick Leeson. It may be a \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  desk bureaucrat with too much discretion and not enough supervision or it may be a senior bureau staffer who hopes to be the Hermann Meier (Austria's skiing phenom aka the Herminator) of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Regardless the actions are reckless, distortionary and could create broad financial market contagion if unchecked. Surely the type of behavior exhibited by MoF on March 05 was not the intent of the Boca Raton G7 communique. And it is an insult to intelligence to suggest otherwise.  \par \par \uc2 -By David Gilmore, \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics; 860-767-9102; fxa@fxa.com  \par \par \uc2 (David Gilmore is an economist and partner at \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics, Essex, Conn., which advises institutional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market participants on a real-time basis. Neither the author nor \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics trades in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. Opinions expressed are those of the author, and not of Dow Jones Newswires.) [ 03-15-04 1618ET ]  \par \par \uc2 1057 4166  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040315e03f0003b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shpp