\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar on defensive after  \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention \highlight\uc2  news.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Gertrude Chavez  \par \uc2 850 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 20:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, March 15 (Reuters) - The dollar was on shaky ground Monday, as news \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is considering scaling back its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy added to dollar-negative variables, like last week's bombings in Spain, already pushing the dollar lower.  \par \par \uc2 A less aggressive Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy would mean the dollar would lose major support going forward, at a time when last week's bombings in Madrid has led some investors to sell dollars and buy 'safer' \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Swiss franc.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar dropped over 1 percent to 109.48 yen after a report from Japanese news wire Nikkei said Japanese officials are considering ending large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the end of March. The dollar trimmed some of its losses against the yen, down 0.4 percent, to trade at around 110.38 yen.  \par \par \uc2 The one dollar-positive variable in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Monday, a report from the U.S. Treasury showing stronger-than-expected capital inflows into the United States in January, proved to be short-lived.  \par \par \uc2 No sooner had the the data hit the market and the dollar began retracing losses suffered earlier in the session, then analysts' began saying such strength in flows was not sustainable, undercutting trader views the report suggested a strong appetite for dollar-dennominated assets.  \par \par \uc2 The capital inflows data have taken on higher importance for the dollar given the pressing need to the United States to attract hefty inflows to finance its wide current account deficit, and the number looms large on traders' radar screens.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders monitor capital flows data as a measure of appetite among international investors for U.S. assets.  \par \par \uc2 Geopolitical tensions caused by bombings in Madrid last week in which 200 died still dogged the dollar, given fears in the market of possible further attacks. This is perceived as negative for the dollar as this meant a flight to safe-haven assets like the Swiss franc.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. net capital inflows climbed to $100.2 billion in January, the highest since May 2003, from $80.8 billion in December. Markets were expecting inflows of around $80 billion for January.  \par \par \uc2 Net foreign purchases of U.S. fixed-income assets rose to $46.9 billion from $29.8 billion in December. But the bulk of U.S. Treasury purchases, came from official or government accounts, specifically from Japan, which showed a rise in Treasury holdings of about $31.8 billion to $577.1 billion from $545.3 billion.  \par \par \uc2 Japan buy U.S. Treasuries as part of its yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for export advantage.  \par \par \uc2 "Net, net, the inflows were sort of a mixed bag than what at first glance was quite positive. And you've got to look at it and break it down and say how much of this was Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Lauren Germain, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of America.  \par \par \uc2 By early afternoon in New York, the euro was up around 0.2 percent against the dollar at $1.2247 . The dollar was down about 0.5 percent at 1.2769 Swiss francs . Sterling was trading nearly flat at $1.8005 .  \par \par \uc2 STOCK PURCHASES, GEOPOLITICAL TENSIONS  \par \par \uc2 Foreign stock purchases dropped to $12.8 billion from $13.3 billion, which analysts said added to the uncertainty about the U.S. capital flow picture.  \par \par \uc2 Strong U.S. capital flows gave the greenback an initial push against the euro, because more dollar purchases meant additional funds to cover the burgeoning U.S. current account deficit - broadly a measure of a nation's global trade.  \par \par \uc2 Michael Woolfolk, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of New York, said this was the third consecutive month that capital flow figures reached levels that were double the amounts needed to cover the monthly US current account deficit of $45.2 million per month.  \par \par \uc2 Overall, markets remained wary of global security tensions. "It has now become a quiet, rangebound market. But certainly markets are on the lookout for some terrorist activity," said Ralph DelZenero, vice president for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Bank One in Chicago.  \par \par \uc2 Traders had sold dollars earlier in Asia and Europe after news Spanish officials had received a videotape purportedly from al-Qaeda claiming responsibility for the Madrid bombings.  \par \par \uc2 Spain's prime minister-elect Jose Luis Rodriguez Zapatero of the opposition Socialist party, who has criticized the former Spanish administration's support for U.S. foreign policy, vowed on Monday to make good on a campaign promise to pull troops out of Iraq.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said this would be a heavy blow to the U.S. administration's efforts to maintain international support for its Iraq campaign and should be negative for the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 A manufacturing survey by the New York Federal Reserve earlier showed a sharp drop in its business conditions index to 25.33 in March from a record high of 42.05 in February, causing an initial spike in the euro.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. industrial production rose by a stronger-than-expected 0.7 percent in February, compared with market expectations of a 0.4 percent rise, but this had little impact on the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040316e03f000r8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
