\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar slips on report  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  may end  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 .  \b0\par\par\uc2 By John Parry  \par \uc2 682 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 22:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, March 15 (Reuters) - The dollar retreated on Monday as a report that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is considering scaling back \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  added to dollar-negative variables like last week's bombings in Spain pressuring the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 A less aggressive Japanese market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy would mean the dollar would lose major support going forward at a time when the Madrid blasts have led some investors to sell dollars and buy 'safer' \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Swiss franc.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar dropped over 1 percent after a report from Japanese news wire Nikkei said Japanese officials are mulling ending large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the end of March.  \par \par \uc2 A U.S. Treasury Department spokesman said Washington's policy in support of a strong dollar was unchanged and declined comment on the Nikkei report.  \par \par \uc2 "The moment they (the Bank of Japan) point to an exit strategy it seems like a one-way street to more yen strength," said Lara Rhame, senior economist with Brown Brothers Harriman in New York.  \par \par \uc2 But Rhame added that it was unclear from the Nikkei report just how likely \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  tactics are to change soon. Traders remain wary about the possibility of continuing yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect exporters.  \par \par \uc2 Late afternoon in New York, the dollar trimmed some of its losses against the yen, down 0.5 percent, to trade at around 110.29 yen .  \par \par \uc2 The one positive for the dollar on Monday, a report from the U.S. Treasury showing stronger-than-expected capital inflows into the United States in January, proved short-lived.  \par \par \uc2 No sooner had the data hit the market and the dollar began retracing losses suffered earlier in the session, than analysts began saying such strength in flows was not sustainable, undercutting trader views the report suggested a strong appetite for dollar-denominated assets.  \par \par \uc2 The capital inflows data have taken on higher importance for the dollar given the pressing need for the United States to attract hefty inflows to finance its wide current account deficit, and the number looms large on traders' radar screens.  \par \par \uc2 Geopolitical tensions caused by the Madrid bombings in which 200 died still dogged the dollar, given fears of further attacks. This is perceived as negative for the dollar as this meant a flight to safe-haven assets like the Swiss franc.  \par \par \uc2 Foreigners' net purchases of U.S. assets rose sharply to $100.2 billion in January from $80.8 billion in December, the highest inflow since May, 2003, Treasury said on Monday, driven partly by buying from foreign central banks.  \par \par \uc2 Foreigners' net purchases of U.S. Treasury bonds and notes were $46.9 billion, up from a net $29.8 billion in December.  \par \par \uc2 The bulk of U.S. Treasury purchases came from official or government accounts, specifically from Japan. Japan buys U.S. Treasuries with proceeds from its yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Late afternoon in New York, the euro was up around 0.4 percent against the dollar at $1.2271 . The dollar was down about 0.7 percent at 1.2750 Swiss francs . Sterling was trading up 0.1 percent at $1.8056 .  \par \par \uc2 Strong U.S. capital flows gave the greenback an initial push against the euro, because more dollar purchases meant additional funds to cover the burgeoning U.S. current account deficit - broadly a measure of a nation's global trade.  \par \par \uc2 Michael Woolfolk, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of New York, said this was the third consecutive month that capital flow figures reached levels that were double the amounts needed to cover the monthly U.S. current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 Overall, markets remained wary of global security tensions. "It has now become a quiet, rangebound market. But certainly markets are on the lookout for some terrorist activity," said Ralph DelZenero, vice president for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Bank One in Chicago.  \par \par \uc2 Traders had sold dollars earlier in Asia and Europe after news Spanish officials had received a videotape purporting to be from al-Qaeda and claiming responsibility for the Madrid bombings. (Additional reporting by Gertrude Chavez in New York).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040315e03f0031o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
