\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Foreign Jan US asset buys highest since May, 2003.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By John Parry  \par \uc2 605 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 20:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, March 15 (Reuters) - Foreigners' net purchases of U.S. assets rose sharply to $100.2 billion in January from $80.8 billion in December, the highest inflow since May, 2003, the U.S. Treasury Department said on Monday, driven partly by buying from foreign central banks.  \par \par \uc2 Subtracting the $8.2 billion net that U.S. investors bought of foreign assets in January, that amounts to a net capital flow into the United States of $92.0 billion, the Treasury said.  \par \par \uc2 The capital flows data have taken on increasing importance for the dollar given the pressing need for the United States to attract hefty inflows to finance its wide current account deficit, and the number looms large on traders' radar screens.  \par \par \uc2 The January data "is overwhelmingly positive for the U.S. dollar today as it shows that the decline in U.S. interest rates to four decade lows has not undermined foreign appetite for U.S. securities to the degree thought earlier," wrote Michael Woolfolk, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with the Bank of New York, in a research note.  \par \par \uc2 The inflow was roughly double the amount needed to cover the monthly U.S. current account deficit, Woolfolk noted. Along with low U.S. interest rates, the wide current account deficit is one of the biggest weights on the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar initially firmed in the wake of the data.  \par \par \uc2 Foreigners' net purchases of U.S. Treasury bonds and notes were $46.9 billion in the month of January, up from $29.8 billion of net purchases in December, according to monthly international capital flows data released by the Treasury Department.  \par \par \uc2 The inflow to U.S. assets "continues to be spurred by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases in U.S. Treasuries," said Ashraf Laidi, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst with MG Financial in New York.  \par \par \uc2 "I think the...data is skewed. The greatest jump was foreign institutional purchases of Treasuries, which everyone knows correlates to the BOJ (Bank of Japan)" said Joe Francomano, vice president of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Erste Bank in New York.  \par \par \uc2 Among those foreign institutions, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders are intently focused on just how willing \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is to continue its hefty \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  designed to weaken the yen. Should those \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  become toned down, that could pare back foreign inflows into U.S. assets, particularly Treasuries and U.S. agency debt.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar fell swiftly against the yen on a report by the Nihon Keizai Shimbun from Tokyo in its early Tuesday edition that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is mulling an end to its massive yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by the end of March.  \par \par \uc2 While central banks accounted for more than half of the net purchases of U.S. Treasuries, private sector investors' net purchases of Treasuries were also substantial, Laidi noted.  \par \par \uc2 Foreigners' made net purchases of U.S. equities of $12.8 billion in January, down from $13.33 billion of net purchases in December, the data showed.  \par \par \uc2 Foreigners net purchases of U.S. corporate bonds in January were $12.98 billion in January, down from $20.38 billion in December.  \par \par \uc2 Foreign accounts' made net purchases of long term U.S. agency bonds of $27.55 billion in January, up from $17.34 billion net purchases in December.  \par \par \uc2 The data for agency bonds underscores the trend seen in the foreign holdings data reported weekly by the Federal Reserve. It shows persistently heavy demand for higher-yielding assets from foreign central banks investing the dollars amassed in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Daniel Bases, Wayne Cole and Lynn Adler in New York).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040315e03f00262\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
