\par\par \b \uc2 Nikkei higher, led by firms reliant on Japan demand.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Risa Maeda  \par \uc2 635 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 04:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 15 (Reuters) - Japanese stocks were higher by midsession on Monday as a rebound on Wall Street encouraged investors to look for bargains after last week's sell-off, in particular among issues dependent on domestic demand.  \par \par \uc2 Construction, retail and real estate firms, seen as some of the main gainers from Japan's economic recovery, drew buyers after dropping in line with the general market last week due to renewed security concerns triggered by the bombings in Madrid.  \par \par \uc2 Foreign investors were particularly active, and the Nikkei average ended the morning session up 1.08 percent at 11,282.82 after touching 11,348.40.  \par \par \uc2 The broader TOPIX index was up 1.34 percent at 1,122.26 by midday.  \par \par \uc2 Last week, the Nikkei lost 3.25 percent, its biggest weekly fall in four months, retreating with other major equity markets around the globe.  \par \par \uc2 "The main reason behind the rebound this morning is New York," said Koji Muneoka, head of domestic sales trading at HSBC Securities.  \par \par \uc2 "But the momentum doesn't look so strong given the market is entering a correction phase (after hitting a 21-month closing high on March 5)."  \par \par \uc2 Several exporters, such as consumer electronics giant Sony Corp, also gained ground. But jitters about global security remained, curbing aggressive buying, analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 Over the weekend a purported spokesman for the Islamic militant group al Qaeda claimed responsibility for last week's Spanish bombings in a videotaped statement.  \par \par \uc2 Another focus this week is a policy meeting by the U.S. Federal Reserve on Tuesday. A rate increase this year is looking less likely given the sluggish job market in the world's biggest economy, which is Japan's main trading partner.  \par \par \uc2 "Investors may start hitting the sidelines ahead of the Fed meeting. It's important for us in two aspects - when and if a rate hike is possible there and if (Fed Chairman Alan) Greenspan steps up the rhetoric on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Toshihiko Matsuno, senior strategist at SMBC Friend Securities.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, Greenspan issued a strong warning about Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases of U.S. assets after they had accumulated dollars through \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , saying it could raise problems for the economies of China and Japan.  \par \par \uc2 Japan has been a heavy buyer of dollars, aiming to prevent the yen from rising in order to protect its exports.  \par \par \uc2 ITO-YOKADO SPIKES  \par \par \uc2 Among retailers, Ito-Yokado Co, Japan's biggest retail group, rose nearly five percent to 4,350 yen on news that it would create a joint venture with a Chinese firm in September to open supermarkets in China's vast consumer market, beginning with an outlet to be set up in Beijing by early 2005.  \par \par \uc2 General contractor Shimizu Corp climbed 4.23 percent to 542 yen and rival Taisei Corp rose 2.26 percent to 407 yen. Mitsubishi Estate Co, Japan's second-biggest real estate firm, gained 3.63 percent to 1,371 yen.  \par \par \uc2 Airlines, hit last week on concerns that travel could take a battering due to security concerns, bounced back. Japan Airlines System Corp, Asia's biggest airline, was up 3.28 percent at 346 yen, recovering from Friday's 2.33 percent fall.  \par \par \uc2 Brokerages ISECU.were the best-performing sector, rising more than three percent after a recent surge in volume.  \par \par \uc2 Industry leader Nomura Holdings added 2.66 percent to reach 1,815 yen and Internet brokerage Matsui Securities Co climbed 4.23 percent to 3,200 yen.  \par \par \uc2 However, volume edged down after Friday's total reached 2.39 billion shares, the highest since October 1988. By midday, 818.77 million shares had changed hands versus 1.65 billion on Friday morning. Advancers outnumbered decliners 1,251 to 206.  \par \par \uc2 . (C) Reuters Limited 2004.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040315e03f0008f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
