\par\par \b \uc2 Ranks Of Dollar Bears Thin As Banks Reassess Outlook  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Tom Barkley  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 760 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 19:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The cracks are widening in the consensus view that the dollar's recent recovery is just a temporary reprieve from a longer-term downtrend, as several major banks have raised their forecasts for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 While the majority of leading institutions involved in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market stick by their views that the dollar is headed lower over the next 12 months, others have revisited their predictions in recent days.  \par \par \uc2 The emerging dissent holds that while more aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will probably only delay the inevitable strengthening of the yen, the euro's dramatic rise to record highs has just about played its course.  \par \par \uc2 On Thursday, Morgan Stanley and ABN Amro each raised their targets for the dollar, giving special mention to policy shift by central banks in the euro zone and Japan.  \par \par \uc2 The BOJ, through action, and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , through verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , have each signaled their resistance to further dollar weakness. With expectations that the euro-zone economy will lag the recovery in the U.S. and Japan, the longer-term outlook for the dollar is for a weakening euro and stronger yen, say the banks.  \par \par \uc2 "The big disparity we have seen in the dollar correction has occurred between European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Japanese yen," said Stephen Jen, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Morgan Stanley in London. "If the U.S. continues to recover, we're going to see a further correction in the dollar index coming through versus the yen."  \par \par \uc2 The recent shift to a "passively offensive" \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , now buying dollars on rallies and not just dips, has put downward pressure on both the yen and the euro against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said. The BOJ, acting on behalf of the Japan's Finance Ministry, has stepped up its already record-setting levels of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks, and that is expected to continue into Japan's March 31 fiscal year end.  \par \par \uc2 Once the BOJ backs off, the euro could still benefit from renewed dollar weakness against the yen, said Jen. But weak euro-zone fundamentals, along with a threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the ECB, should push the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  back down below current levels by the end of the year.  \par \par \uc2 Morgan Stanley raised its forecast for the dollar to Y113 from Y106 in three months, Y107 from Y103 in six months, and Y105 from Y102 by year end. It changed its outlook for the euro to $1.19 from $1.25 in three months, $1.25 from $1.24 in six months, and back down to $1.20 from $1.23 by 2005.  \par \par \uc2 ABN Amro also believes a weak euro-zone economy and the threat of ECB action means that the euro's run is almost over, but that dollar-negative factors such as the difficulty to attract enough foreign investment to fund the U.S. current account deficit could result in new highs for the euro around $1.30 within the next few months.  \par \par \uc2 Any resurgence in the euro is expected to be short-lived, however, as the market starts pricing in monetary policy shifts by the Federal Reserve and ECB. ABN Amro predicts a 50-basis-point cut by the ECB in September and an opposite move by the Fed two months later, which will eliminate the euro-zone's interest-rate advantage.  \par \par \uc2 "Our belief is that the ECB will be cutting rates by the end of the year because the European economy won't recover in line with Asia and the U.S.," said Greg Anderson, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro in Chicago. "That prevents the euro from going any higher, and toward the end of the year it will probably stabilize around where it is now."  \par \par \uc2 ABN Amro lowered its target for the euro to $1.30 from $1.33 in three months, $1.23 from $1.33 in six months, and $1.20 from $1.27 in 12 months. The dollar's outlook against the yen improved to Y108 from Y103 in three months and to Y105 from Y100 in six and 12 months.  \par \par \uc2 JPMorgan also tweaked its outlook for the dollar on Thursday, citing stepped up activity by the BOJ. The bank raised its month-end forecast to Y110, though it still expects the dollar to fall to Y99 by year end.  \par \par \uc2 -By Tom Barkley, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4385  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     tom.barkley@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 03-12-04 1320ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040312e03c00039\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
