\par\par \b \uc2 Japan Tanigaki:Watching  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Carefully, Will Act As Needed  \b0\par\par\uc2 209 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 04:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan will continue to monitor \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market movements and take action as needed, Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said Thursday, underscoring Tokyo's resolve to hobble the yen.  \par \par \uc2 "With regard to \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , we believe it is important that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates reflect economic fundamentals and move stably," Tanigaki said, reading a prepared speech before an Upper House fiscal and financial affairs committee.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily to reverse the dollar's fall against the yen since late last year, claiming a rise in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is out of line with economic fundamentals. A strong yen hurts Japan's economy by making exports less competitive overseas and eroding the yen-denominated earnings of major manufacturers.  \par \par \uc2 The MOF has spent more than Y10 trillion selling yen in the first to months of this year, and is reported to have spent trillions more this month even as the dollar rebounds against the yen - \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a way which traders say appears to be aimed at eliminating all speculative bets from the market.  \par \par \uc2 -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; miyako.takebe@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Alastair Gale [ 11-03-04 0324GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040311e03b0003h\par }\pag