\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar advances in technically driven session.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Gertrude Chavez  \par \uc2 601 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 23:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, March 10 (Reuters) - The dollar strengthened against some major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday in a technically driven session as investors took profits on other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "A lot of this has to do with the very natural technical correction from such extremely lofty levels achieved by such \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the Australian dollar and sterling over the last three months. There's natural profit-taking going on," said Joe Francomano, vice president for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Erste Bank in New York.  \par \par \uc2 By late afternoon in New York, the euro was down sharply against the yen at 135.50 yen . Against the dollar, the euro fell 0.7 percent to $1.2231 .  \par \par \uc2 The euro has shed about seven cents since hitting record highs against the dollar above $1.2900 in mid-February.  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, however, the dollar was down about 0.4 percent to 110.78 yen . Investors remained on alert for yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese monetary authorities, although an unexpected downward revision in Japan's growth data for October-December alleviated pressure on the yen.  \par \par \uc2 Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was up about 0.4 percent at 1.2867 francs . Sterling was down more than 1 percent at $1.8031, while the Australian dollar dropped steeply, to US$0.7457 .  \par \par \uc2 "It's tough to find a clear-cut macro story for the dollar's rally," said Daniel Tenengauzer, vice president for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Lehman Brothers in New York.  \par \par \uc2 He said investors are unwinding their previously held long positions in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the euro, yen, and sterling and "basically taking some risk off the table."  \par \par \uc2 Traders said they were wary of buying the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after it slipped below a key technical area against the dollar between $1.2330 and $1.2350 in the prior session.  \par \par \uc2 Erste's Francomano said for the rest of the week, markets would be focused more on the fundamentals of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  other than the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 What investors have found in the last few sessions is that other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' fundamentals are not a strong as perceived, he said, referring specifically to the the UK's record trade deficit for January, which has fueled doubts about the strength of the UK economy.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier, the U.S. January trade deficit came in at $43.01 billion, compared with a revised December deficit of $42.69 billion. Economists' median forecasts had been for a January trade deficit of $42.05 billion.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar had a brief sell-off against the euro after the trade data, but the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  subsequently retreated against the greenback.  \par \par \uc2 "Within the more long-term cycle in the dollar bear market, we are seeing a counter-trend rally in the dollar. Sometimes, during these periods, the underlying fundamentals move to the background," said Paresh Upadhyaya, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst with Putnam Investments in Boston.  \par \par \uc2 In Europe, European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governing Council member Ernst Welteke said the German economy was showing signs of recovering, but only at a very sluggish pace. He said the country's economic trend depended more on global growth than exchange rates.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier, Japan said its economy grew 1.6 percent in the October-December period from the previous quarter, revising down its initial reading of 1.7 percent.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday after the dollar tumbled toward 110 yen. Money market analysts in Tokyo estimated \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  probably \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the tune of 500 billion yen ($4.50 billion). There was no confirmation from the authorities.  \par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by John Parry in New York).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040310e03a003xz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
