\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar rallies in technically-driven trade.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Gertrude Chavez  \par \uc2 541 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 20:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, March 10 (Reuters) - The dollar climbed against some major counterparts on Wednesday in technically-driven trading as markets shrugged off a wider-than-expected U.S. January trade deficit and continued to take profits on previous long positions in other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "Long" positions are essentially bets that a specific \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will rise.  \par \par \uc2 "It's tough to find a clear-cut macro story for the dollar's rally. This is technically-driven more than anything else," said Daniel Tenengauzer, vice president for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Lehman Brothers in New York.  \par \par \uc2 He added that investors are unwinding their previously-held long positions in other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the euro, yen, and sterling and "basically taking some risk off the table."  \par \par \uc2 Traders said they were wary of buying the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after it slipped below a key technical area against the dollar between $1.2330 and $1.2350 in the prior session.  \par \par \uc2 That paved the way for the euro's fall on Wednesday, and the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also crashed below support against the yen.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By midafternoon in New York, the euro was down sharply against the yen\par at 135.58 yen. Against the dollar, the euro        fell 0.7 percent to\par $1.2231.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro has shed about seven cents since hitting record highs against the dollar above $1.2900 in mid-February.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Against the yen , however, the dollar was down about 0.4 percent to 110.83 yen. Investors remained on alert for yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by Japanese monetary authorities, although an unexpected downward revision in Japan's growth data for October-December alleviated pressure on the yen.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Against the Swiss franc , the dollar was up about 0.5 percent to 1.2886 francs. Sterling was down over 1 percent to $1.8041.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There's complete, utter lack of liquidity and direction at the moment. Not a whole lot of risk appetite there," said a New York-based trader.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Earlier, the U.S. January trade deficit came in at $43.06 billion, compared with a revised December deficit of $42.69 billion. Economists' median forecasts had been for a January trade deficit of $42.05 billion.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After a brief bounce higher, the euro retreated against the dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Within the more long-term cycle in the dollar bear market, we are seeing a counter-trend rally in the dollar. Sometimes, during these periods, the underlying fundamentals move to the background," said Paresh Upadhyaya, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analyst with Putnam Investments in Boston.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Europe, European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Governing Council member Ernst Welteke said the German economy was showing signs of recovering, but only at a very sluggish pace. He said the country's economic trend depended more on global growth than exchange rates.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Earlier, Japan said the economy grew 1.6 percent in the October-December period from the previous quarter, revising down its initial reading of 1.7 percent.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had been suspected of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Tuesday after the dollar tumbled toward 110 yen. Money market analysts in Tokyo estimated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  probably \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to the tune of 500 billion yen ($4.50 billion). There was no confirmation from the authorities.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Additional reporting by John Parry in New York).   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040310e03a003ky\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
