\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 1-U.S. stocks drop - bonds, dollar and silver rally.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Carol Bishopric  \par \uc2 866 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 01:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, March 9 (Reuters) - U.S. stocks fell on Tuesday, driving the tech-heavy Nasdaq below 2,000 to its lowest close this year, but bonds, the dollar and silver rallied.   \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury bond prices climbed for the third straight day, after falling stocks reinforced the perception that any increase in interest rates is far off in the future.   \par \par \uc2  The dollar rebounded against the euro and the yen after suspected Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars in an effort to weaken the yen. A weaker yen makes Japanese exporters' goods less expensive, especially in the United States.   \par \par \uc2  In New York, silver prices hit a 16-year high as big speculators bought on the idea that silver is still cheap relative to gold. Gold also rose, while oil fell.   \par \par \uc2  The Nasdaq Composite Index lost 13.62 points, or 0.68 percent, to 1,995.16, its lowest finish since Dec. 26, 2003. The Dow Jones industrial average ended down 72.52 points, or 0.69 percent, at 10,456.96, its lowest close since mid-January. The broad Standard & Poor's 500 Index gave up 6.62 points, or 0.58 percent, to 1,140.58, its lowest close since late February.   \par \par \uc2  "There's a psychological value with (the Nasdaq) dipping below the 2,000 mark, causing traders to feel uncomfortable," said Fred Dickson, market strategist at brokerage D.A. Davidson in Portland, Oregon.   \par \par \uc2  The Nasdaq hit a lifetime closing high of 5,048.62 on March 10, 2000.   \par \par \uc2  A positive forecast from Proctor & Gamble Co., a Dow component, could give blue-chip stocks a lift at Wednesday's open.   \par \par \uc2  After the close of regular trading, P&G raised its third quarter and fiscal 2004 profit outlook, announced a two-for-one stock split and lifted its annual dividend. In after-hours trading, its stock rose to $104 from $102.49 at the close of New York Stock Exchange Trading.   \par \par \uc2  BONDS AND DOLLAR RALLY   \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury bond yields fell Tuesday to eight-month lows for the third consecutive day as prices climbed on hopes the Fed would keep interest rates low for a long time - after Friday's weaker-than-expected February jobs data.   \par \par \uc2  The benchmark 10-year Treasury note rose 12/32 to 102-9/32, dropping its yield to 3.725 percent from 3.77 percent late on Monday and sharply below its level near 4.02 percent before Friday morning's jobs report.   \par \par \uc2  The 30-year bond jumped 21/32 to 110-23/32 to yield 4.67 percent, down from 4.71 percent on Monday.   \par \par \uc2  The two-year Treasury note rose 1/32 to 100-09/32, to yield 1.488 percent, dipping under 1.50 percent for the first time since early October.   \par \par \uc2  In late U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading, the dollar rose 0.17 percent against the Japanese yen to 111.35 yen from 111.16 late Monday in New York.   \par \par \uc2  Early Tuesday afternoon, the dollar fell briefly to as low as 110.25 yen after traders said the Bank of Japan had withdrawn its bids for the dollar. Later Tuesday, the dollar rose to 111.41 yen after it appeared that Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reinstated its bids to buy dollars and weaken the yen.   \par \par \uc2  The euro fell 0.73 percent to $1.2311 from $1.2402 on Monday.   \par \par \uc2  "The intense support of dollar/yen by the BoJ is starting to filter into broader support for the dollar," said Richard Franulovich, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at WestPac Banking Corp in New York.   \par \par \uc2  Sterling  fell 1.29 percent to $1.8255.   \par \par \uc2  SILVER HITS 16-YEAR HIGH   \par \par \uc2  At the COMEX in New York, silver for May delivery rose to $7.20 an ounce before the close, the highest price for a benchmark contract since the middle of 1988. The contract closed up 19 cents at $7.18.   \par \par \uc2  Some traders attributed the rise to technical buying by large commodity funds and speculation that the price of silver was still too low relative to gold.   \par \par \uc2  April gold settled up $3.60, or 0.9 percent, at $404.50 an ounce. The gold contract has underperformed silver since setting a 15-year high on Jan. 6 at $432.30.   \par \par \uc2  On the New York Mercantile Exchange, April crude fell 29 cents to settle at $36.28 per barrel, while NYMEX April gasoline settled down 2.42 cents at $1.0593 a gallon.   \par \par \uc2  Crude oil futures prices declined in sync with the drop in gasoline futures, which traders attributed to fears that high retail gasoline prices would curb demand.   \par \par \uc2  In overseas trading, the FTSE Eurotop 300 index of pan-European blue chips fell 1.1 percent to close at 1015.3, its worst one-day point decline since last November.   \par \par \uc2  Japan's benchmark Nikkei 225 average closed Tuesday at 11,532.04 up 0.25 percent. Japan's TOPIX  hit a fresh 21-month high, rising 0.20 percent to end at 1,134.34, its highest since May 24, 2002. (Reporting by Bill Rigby, Vivian Chu, Pedro Nicolaci da Costa, Gertrude Chavez, Robert Gibbons and Alden Bentley).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040310e03a0003c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
