\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 1-U.S. stocks drop - bonds, dollar and silver rally.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Carol Bishopric  \par \uc2 804 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 01:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, March 9 (Reuters) - U.S. stocks fell on Tuesday, driving the tech-heavy Nasdaq below 2,000 to its lowest close this year, but bonds, the dollar and silver rallied.   \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury bond prices climbed for the third straight day, after falling stocks reinforced the perception that any increase in interest rates is far off in the future.   \par \par \uc2  The dollar rebounded against the euro and the yen after suspected Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars in an effort to weaken the yen. A weaker yen makes Japanese exporters' goods less expensive, especially in the United States.   \par \par \uc2  In New York, silver prices hit a 16-year high as big speculators bought on the idea that silver is still cheap relative to gold. Gold also rose, while oil fell.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Nasdaq Composite Index  lost 13.62 points, or 0.68 percent, to\par 1,995.16, its lowest finish since Dec. 26, 2003. The Dow Jones\par industrial average        ended down 72.52 points, or 0.69 percent, at\par 10,456.96, its lowest close since mid-January. The broad Standard &\par Poor's 500 Index        gave up 6.62 points, or 0.58 percent, to\par 1,140.58, its lowest close since late February.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "There's a psychological value with (the Nasdaq) dipping below the\par 2,000 mark, causing traders to feel uncomfortable," said Fred Dickson,\par market strategist at brokerage D.A. Davidson in Portland, Oregon.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Nasdaq hit a lifetime closing high of 5,048.62 on March 10, 2000.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A positive forecast from Proctor & Gamble Co. (PG.N), a Dow component,\par could give blue-chip stocks a lift at Wednesday's open.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par After the close of regular trading, P&G raised its third quarter and\par fiscal 2004 profit outlook, announced a two-for-one stock split and\par lifted its annual dividend. In after-hours trading, its stock rose to\par $104 from $102.49 at the close of New York Stock Exchange Trading.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BONDS AND DOLLAR RALLY\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. Treasury bond yields fell Tuesday to eight-month lows for the\par third consecutive day as prices climbed on hopes the Fed would keep\par interest rates low for a long time - after Friday's\par weaker-than-expected February jobs data.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The benchmark 10-year Treasury note  rose 12/32 to 102-9/32, dropping\par its yield to 3.725 percent from 3.77 percent late on Monday and sharply\par below its level near 4.02 percent before Friday morning's jobs report.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The 30-year bond  jumped 21/32 to 110-23/32 to yield 4.67 percent, down\par from 4.71 percent on Monday.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The two-year Treasury note  rose 1/32 to 100-09/32, to yield 1.488\par percent, dipping under 1.50 percent for the first time since early\par October.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In late U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading, the dollar rose 0.17 percent against the\par Japanese yen  to 111.35 yen from 111.16 late Monday in New York.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Early Tuesday afternoon, the dollar fell briefly to as low as 110.25\par yen after traders said the Bank of Japan had withdrawn its bids for the\par dollar. Later Tuesday, the dollar rose to 111.41 yen after it appeared\par that Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reinstated its bids to buy dollars and weaken\par the yen.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The euro  fell 0.73 percent to $1.2311 from $1.2402 on Monday.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The intense support of dollar/yen by the BoJ is starting to filter\par into broader support for the dollar," said Richard Franulovich, senior\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at WestPac Banking Corp in New York.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Sterling  fell 1.29 percent to $1.8255.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SILVER HITS 16-YEAR HIGH\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par At the COMEX in New York, silver for May delivery rose to $7.20 an\par ounce before the close, the highest price for a benchmark contract\par since the middle of 1988. The contract closed up 19 cents at $7.18.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Some traders attributed the rise to technical buying by large commodity\par funds and speculation that the price of silver was still too low\par relative to gold.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On the New York Mercantile Exchange, April crude  fell 29 cents to\par settle at $36.28 per barrel, while NYMEX April gasoline        settled\par down 2.42 cents at $1.0593 a gallon.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Crude oil futures prices declined in sync with the drop in gasoline\par futures, which traders attributed to fears that high retail gasoline\par prices would curb demand.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japan's benchmark Nikkei 225  average closed Tuesday at 11,532.04 up\par 0.25 percent. Japan's TOPIX         hit a fresh 21-month high, rising\par 0.20 percent to end at 1,134.34, its highest since May 24, 2002.\par (Reporting by Bill Rigby, Vivian Chu, Pedro Nicolaci da Costa, Gertrude\par Chavez, Robert Gibbons and Alden Bentley).\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040310e03a00036\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
