\par\par \b \uc2 FXAnalytics Focus:MoF's Weapon Of Mass Fincl Destruction  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,700 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 22:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By David Gilmore \par    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Analytics \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Since the Boca Raton G7 meeting, two important developments have occurred in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. One is that the dollar's two-year downtrend has stalled, and partly reversed (not the first time in the trend to do so...see June-September 2003), and two, Japan's government has rewritten the record books for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation surpassing anything ever before seen by a major or minor \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  in sheer volume of trades and sheer audacity short of instituting capital controls. Japan for the better part of two years has become the dominant single force in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by a factor of a hundred (well thereabouts anyway). And for what? To fight deflation...keep the yen from rising (Japan runs a large current surplus and exports very little private capital) and exports rising. Clyde Prestowitz an economic adviser to John Kerry, the expected Democratic presidential nominee, called the policy protectionist, a subsidy to exporters and something Kerry, if elected, will change.  \par \par \uc2 How did Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with the support of the Bank of Japan, ever get to the point it did last Friday where it essentially spent anywhere from $10-20bln (range of estimates) to prevent the dollar from weakening against the yen after a disappointing U.S. payroll result? Indeed, rather than weaken, as would have happened if market forces were allowed to play out, dollar/yen rose in value by 1.2% (euro rose 2.0% versus the dollar). And MoF has been engineering the move up in dollar/yen since mid-February from 105 by buying loads of dollars at increasingly higher levels, training the market to bet with it.  \par \par \uc2 Have U.S. or Japan fundamentals changed much since February? To some extent yes. Japan's economy is looking increasingly healthy...finally it may be on the path for sustained growth. Exports are holding up, business investment is showing signs of life and most importantly consumers are becoming restless. Japan's stock market is also taking notice, with the Nikkei 225 at a two-year high. Darn it but this tells me the yen should be strengthening. And what about U.S. fundamentals? I have already noted the very disappointing February non-farm payroll growth (just 21,000). Firms for whatever reason (ones no one expected just months ago) are not piling all those profits being recorded into more capacity. And all the tax cuts have failed to prompt firms selling more goods and services, earning more profits from investing in labor and only marginally increasing investment in new capital. Meanwhile the U.S. continues to import more than it exports (at a record share of GDP). It needs ever more foreign savings to finance the investment-savings imbalance. In other words this tells me the dollar should be lower.  \par \par \uc2 Not according to MoF. MoF likes to note that the U.S. is growing at a faster rate than Japan (now on average after Japan's robust 4Q GDP change of 1.6% Q/Q) and hence the dollar should strengthen. Sure at times the relative growth story has dominated the external imbalance story and led to a stronger dollar. Indeed that is the story the market lived and breathed by at the dollar highs two years ago. Well now that we are two years into a weaker dollar and there is little adjustment in the US current account imbalance the adjustment story is implausible, right? Hardly. For any reasonable economist it means absent structural changes such as a higher propensity to save in the US and spend in Japan and the rest of the world, much larger swings in the nominal exchange rate are needed to foster adjustment in the external imbalances. Sure no one is arguing for a free falling, disorderly and disruptive dollar decline. But Japan has no case to justify a forced depreciation of the yen versus the dollar. It does have a case for smoothing the rate of yen appreciation until it is out of the woods on deflation especially when considering the extent to which most of Japan's neighbors and regional trading partners manage their respective \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  versus the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 So it is with this in mind that G7 in early February at Boca Raton agreed to give Japan special dispensation (exclusion) from its call for more flexible exchange rate regimes (in Asia) and burden sharing in the adjustment process. By late February MoF officials had reinterpreted the Boca Raton message to mean not only was Japan excluded from the call for greater flexibility in (Asia's) exchange rate regimes, but Boca dispensation freed MoF up to chase dollar/yen above 115, roughly where it was before the G7 meeting in Dubai last September (also the high point for the dollar before the latest leg down to the February low). Rest assured this was neither the letter nor the intent of the Boca communique.  \par \par \uc2 MoF indeed went postal on Friday and reverse postal on Monday and Tuesday (at 1800GMT). But it is the shift in Japan economic policy from normal monetary and fiscal channels for economic stimulus into one policy channel...keeping the yen from appreciating...that has created far greater distortions than simply slowing the yen rise, stabilizing it and more recently beginning to reverse it. (MoF would argue it has exhausted fiscal stimulus...debt maxed out...and monetary stimulus...zero rates.) Sure weapon of financial mass destruction maybe a bit strong. However, the concept is right. Governments as active as MoF in the largest capital market in the world (two largest if you count US Treasuries), can't be a good thing. Is this really a private capital market? Is \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and US Treasury holdings a drop in the bucket of a vast sea of liquidity? Again hardly. While Greenspan in recent Congressional testimony played down the significance that Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling of U.S. Treasuries would have on interest rates, in his speech to the Economics Club of New York last week he described Japan's accumulation of dollars and Treasuries as awesome. And he said it was an unsustainable policy and perhaps soon to expire. The U.S. bond market has plenty of strength (buyers) and does not need record purchases by Asian central banks at this stage of the recovery/business cycle. Remember Greenspan also warned Congress recently about the systemic risks associated with the Government Sponsored Entities or GSE's (Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac), which the hot property and refinancing market (thank 40-45-year lows in borrowing rates) has helped fuel. Does a 21,000 payroll print in February get Greenspan warm and fuzzy over a new round of refinancing and home buying? He has to be thinking about larger risk that the U.S. credit markets are about as bloated as the waist line of the average American...unsustainable...and best to head for the tread mill sooner than be dragged into the OR later. What does this have to do with Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys U.S. Treasuries (with rest of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  managers of the world it is picking up 35-45% of just about every major government note and debt auction). No one is calling for full stop to Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or U.S. Treasury market support...but the scale needs to be reduced. And if you believe as I do that the credit market is at risk of becoming a bubble, Japan is a source of hot air and one that conceivably can be controlled with maybe something as simple as a phone call.  \par \par \uc2 And then there are the implications from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's unprecedented \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the rest of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. A sudden move up in dollar/yen, arguably initiated and then support by MoF, ignited waves of repositioning in other \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs as risk aversion flooded the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. We could talk about why euro/dollar has fallen in recent weeks and we would be hard pressed to explain it based on fundamentals (I leave this to technical analysts). Sure the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market has itself in part to blame for recent dollar rally...greed led as it always does to a one-sided assessment of risk (dollar could only go lower...I should know I was there too in thought and word). But MoF has a lot to do with the recent gyrations in the dollar versus the euro, Swiss franc, British pound, Australian dollar, and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well as the euro versus the yen, the pound versus the franc, the pound versus the Canadian dollar and so on. One-sided markets are never sustainable, but let's be frank, this time two-way risk was introduced by MoF in dollar/yen and ignited a wave of contagion. One has to ask if this type of behavior is conducive to orderly, non-excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets? Indeed contagion briefly infiltrated the commodities market last Friday (see copper). It did not happen, but what if the bond market was contaminated? Or the stock market? The kind of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducted last Friday is in fact a high stakes activity that could easily spin out of control.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Secretary Snow stressed the need for markets to set exchange rates not governments in his remarks Monday. I doubt this was a coincident, despite what other reputable analysts say to the contrary. Maybe my notion of the distortions \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is generating leans in the direction of overstating. But a strong dose of caution is warranted and like most examples of excessive government manipulation of prices (markets), they end badly. So to the gym for some cardio rather than to the OR for triple bypass.  \par \par \uc2 -By David Gilmore, \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics; 860-767-9102; fxa@fxa.com  \par \par \uc2 (David Gilmore is an economist and partner at \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics, Essex, Conn., which advises institutional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market participants on a real-time basis. Neither the author nor \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics trades in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. Opinions expressed are those of the author, and not of Dow Jones Newswires.) [ 03-10-04 1618ET ]  \par \par \uc2 1057 4166  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040310e03a0006m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
