\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar rebounds, buoyed by suspected BoJ buying.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Gertrude Chavez  \par \uc2 667 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 22:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, March 9 (Reuters) - The dollar rallied against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in volatile trade on Tuesday, drawing support from suspected Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen against the greenback, analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 In a repeat of Monday's performance, the dollar fell briefly against the yen as traders again cited the BoJ withdrawing its offer to buy dollars, dragging the U.S \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down to 110.25 yen from 111.17 in just one minute in early afternoon trade.  \par \par \uc2 The BoJ, which acts on behalf of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen for export advantage, has since reinstated its bids for the dollar, pushing the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 "The market was very long, and the BoJ pulled its bids, executing the stops and put those bids back up again," said a New York-based trader.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the BoJ's removal of its bids for the dollar triggered key stop-losses below 111 yen.  \par \par \uc2 A stop-loss order, placed with a broker to sell when a certain price is reached, is designed to limit an investor's loss on a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  loss or security position.  \par \par \uc2 "The intense support of dollar/yen by the BoJ is starting to filter into broader support for the dollar. It has sort of limited generalized dollar losses across the board," said Richard Franulovich, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at WestPac Banking Corp in New York.  \par \par \uc2 According to traders, the BoJ's removal of its dollar bids may be part of the MoF's deal to allow Japanese exporters to settle their accounts, although normally the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would wait until the end of the month to do so.  \par \par \uc2 In midafternoon New York trade, the euro traded down close to 0.8 percent at $1.2302 after rising to about $1.2461 in Europe. Last week, the euro hit three-month lows around $1.2056. In February, it hit record highs above $1.2900.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar had a late surge against the euro, with some traders citing buying by Japanese banks around 111.15 yen as one of the catalysts.  \par \par \uc2 "It's technically-driven. We hit a lot of stops below $1.2330 and $1.2335," said John McCarthy, director for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at ING Capital Markets in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The euro also slipped close to 0.6 percent against the yen to 136.99 .  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the dollar rose 0.2 percent to 111.41 yen , after falling as low as 110.25 after the BoJ made its move early afternoon. On Monday, the dollar fell around one yen after the BoJ withdrew its bids in the market. traders said.  \par \par \uc2 Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was down 0.6 percent at 1.2818 francs .  \par \par \uc2 Sterling fell more than 1 percent to $1.8268.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier, the euro gained from sterling weakness after a record British trade deficit triggered pound sales.  \par \par \uc2 But the euro and the dollar remain broadly range-bound as traders grapple with a moment of indecision, some said.  \par \par \uc2 "The debate is now whether we have bottomed or whether (this) is a small consolidation before the next move down in the dollar," said Brian Taylor, managing director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading with Manufacturers and Traders Bank in Buffalo, New York.  \par \par \uc2 Investors appeared reluctant to buy the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively even after last week's dismal U.S. jobs data caused analysts to postpone expectations for a Federal Reserve rate hike, which weighed on the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar earlier shrugged off a Federal Reserve Bank of Richmond manufacturing and service index for February, despite a gain in the survey's manufacturing employment component.  \par \par \uc2 With few clear catalysts to trade on, investors are eyeing U.S. reports later in the week including retail sales and data on the wide current account deficit which may determine whether the dollar will rupture current ranges and in which direction. (Additional reporting by John Parry in New York).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040309e039002ur\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
