\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 1-U.S. stocks slip, bonds rally, dollar drops.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Carol Bishopric  \par \uc2 922 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 01:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, March 8 (Reuters) - U.S. stocks fell on Monday, with investors cautious before a mid-quarter update from Texas Instruments Inc., but Treasury bond prices rose.   \par \par \uc2  The dollar hit a five-month high around 112.32 yen and then ended the U.S. session lower against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , deflating quickly on market talk that the Bank of Jaofell against the euro.   \par \par \uc2  In New York, oil and gold prices fell. But oil stayed near $37 a barrel and gold hung on just above $400 an ounce.   \par \par \uc2  Silver hit a six-year peak above $7 an ounce before retreating to end the session unchanged. And palladium hit $267 an ounce - its highest level in more than a year as speculators looked for precious metals to buy while gold languishes.   \par \par \uc2  After the stock market's regular session ended, Texas Instruments raised its first-quarter earnings and revenue targets due to strong demand across a broad product range.   \par \par \uc2  Shares of Texas Instruments, the largest maker of chips for cell phones, slipped in after-hours trading to $30.45, from their close at $30.60 on the New York Stock Exchange.   \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones industrial average ended down 66.07 points, or 0.62 percent, at 10,529.48. The Standard & Poor's 500 index fell 9.66 points, or 0.84 percent, to 1,147.20. The technology-laden Nasdaq Composite Index dropped 38.85 points, or 1.90 percent, to 2,008.78.   \par \par \uc2  CHIP STOCKS ARE DOWN   \par \par \uc2  The Philadelphia Stock Exchange semiconductor index fell 3.76 percent.   \par \par \uc2  Intel Corp., the world's No. 1 computer chipmaker, led technology and blue-chip shares down after IDC, a tech market research firm, forecast modest growth rates in chip sales over the next five years.   \par \par \uc2  The semiconductor sector "has been overvalued for a while, and probably ran ahead of itself," said Edgar Peters, chief investment officer at PanAgora Asset Management in Boston.   \par \par \uc2  Intel shares fell $1.25, or 4.3 percent, to $27.70 and ranked as the blue-chip Dow's biggest percentage loser. Near stagnant spending on desktop computers will hold annual growth rates in microchip sales to single-digit figures through 2008, according to the IDC forecast.   \par \par \uc2  Shares of Martha Stewart's namesake company continued to slide. Martha Stewart Living Omnimedia, the media and merchandising company, fell 86 cents, or 7.9 percent, to $10 following a sharp drop on Friday. Revlon Inc. said on Monday that Stewart is resigning from its board. She was convicted of criminal charges on Friday in connection with her sale of ImClone Systems Inc. stock.   \par \par \uc2  BONDS RALLY   \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury bond prices jumped on Monday, pushing yields to eight-month lows for the second straight day as investors continued to bet that a jobless recovery means the Federal Reserve will refrain from raising interest rates in 2004.   \par \par \uc2  The February employment report, released Friday, jolted financial markets when it showed U.S. non-farm payrolls added only 21,000 jobs last month - sharply below expectations for a gain of 125,000. That prompted bond investors to buy debt and reinforced the hope that the Fed would keep rates low.   \par \par \uc2  "We've taken the Fed out of the market for this year," said Mark Mahoney, Treasury market strategist at UBS.   \par \par \uc2  The price of the two-year note rose 4/32 to 100-8/32, while its yield slipped to 1.50 percent from 1.56 percent late Friday.   \par \par \uc2  The benchmark 10-year note gained 22/32 to 101-28/32, and its yield declined to 3.77 percent from 3.84 percent late Friday. The price of the 30-year bond climbed 27/32 to 110-2/32, while its yield dropped to 4.71 percent from 4.76 percent late Friday.   \par \par \uc2  DOLLAR RETREATS   \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell against the yen and euro on Monday. The dollar retreated from a high against the yen amid market rumors that Japan had withdrawn its offer to buy the greenback after suspected heavy dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier in the day, traders said. However, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to buy dollars again in the next few days to stabilize the yen before Japan's fiscal year ends on March 31.   \par \par \uc2  In late U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading, the euro rose to $1.2403 from $1.2373, up 0.24 percent. The dollar was off 0.79 percent versus the yen, falling to 111.18 yen from 112.06 yen late on Friday.   \par \par \uc2  OIL AND GOLD FALL   \par \par \uc2  Gold prices edged lower after European central banks agreed to limit their bullion sales over the next five years at a slightly higher-than-expected level.   \par \par \uc2  COMEX April gold fell 70 cents to settle at $400.90 an ounce..   \par \par \uc2  COMEX May silver was down most of the day, then popped up to $7.07 an ounce, its highest since February 1998. But May silver gave up those gains, closing unchanged at $6.99.   \par \par \uc2  June palladium surged $14.10 to $267.00 an ounce.   \par \par \uc2  On the New York Mercantile Exchange, April crude oil lost 69 cents to end at $36.57 a barrel. Gasoline futures fell.   \par \par \uc2  Overseas, Britain's FTSE-100 index edged up 6.7 points or 0.15 percent to finish at 4,553.8.   \par \par \uc2  Japan's benchmark Nikkei 225 average slipped 0.30 percent or 34.43 points to close at 11,502.86. (Reporting by Bill Rigby, Vivian Chu, Pedro Nicolaci da Costa, Gertrude Chavez, Robert Gibbons and Alden Bentley).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040309e03900030\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
