\par\par \b \uc2 FXAnalytics Focus: Dollar/Yen Snow Melt?  \b0\par\par\uc2 772 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 14:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By David Gilmore \par    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Analytics \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dollar/yen sank sharply Monday when it became obvious that Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the Bank of Japan (MoF's execution arm) pulled its bid at Y112, allowing large stops to run off. This followed a period of supporting dollar/yen at Y112 since Friday, after the MoF chased it higher from Y111.00 despite a large move down in the dollar versus the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  behind a weak U.S. payroll gain for February.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 What on earth is going on in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? MoF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday was reminiscent in scale (for one day) of the Bank of England's effort in 1992 to keep sterling in the Exchange Rate Mechanism. But Japan faced no such outcome, only a stronger yen on weak U.S. labor market data. Indeed Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (scale of it) flew in the face of the G7 statement at Boca Raton that expressed concern over excessive volatility in exchange markets as well as the broader call for greater flexibility in (Asia's) \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimes. At the time, I can say with high level of confidence, that the U.S. Treasury didn't intend the call for greater flexibility in exchange rates to apply to Japan as Japan faced pressures from the region where most governments actively managed local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar (namely preventing their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from appreciating). Moreover, the context of a weak U.S. labor market and Democrats (John Kerry) challenging the Bush administration's economic policies for failing to produce jobs and exporting jobs it was insulting and surely infuriating for U.S. officials. Friday's behavior by the MoF through the BoJ was nothing short of taking advantage of U.S. largesse, not to mention largesse of Europe as euro/yen soared. Surely Japan's behavior in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market last week went against the spirit, if not the letter, of the Boca statement in exchange markets. As I said Friday, an official rebuke by the U.S. and euro zone was warranted. Could it be that the MoF heard from Snow today?  \par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow today said no \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  can be regarded as strong if it depends on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop it up. He added that no country has ever successfully devalued its way to prosperity. And he stressed that it is best to leave \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels to markets to set, so that imbalances (like the U.S. current account deficit) can adjust. He said if a country exports more than it imports, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should reflect this (converse is true ... the dollar should be weak, China's yuan strong). He noted exchange-rate adjustments in Europe and the lack of them in China. He didn't specify Japan, but surely fill in the blank.  \par \par \uc2 It is too coincidental that after Snow's message on exchange rates (prompted by a reporter's question) has some relevance for Japan in the wake of Japan's shift in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy from smoothing the yen's rise, to stabilizing the yen above Y105, to its current policy of weakening the yen. Snow is surely not eager to embarrass Japan publicly with a rebuke, but the statements and subsequent collapse in dollar/yen suggest a phone call was made to the MoF today from the Bush administration. The message was simple ... our agreement (to look the other way on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) applies to smoothing efforts, not weakening efforts. Moreover, the manner in which Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  occurred created volatility. What else explains why the MoF suddenly pulled its bid in dollar/yen in a thin lackluster session? Again I have no hard proof of this, but one does not have to be Sherlock Holmes to figure out what happened to the MoF's Y112 bid.  \par \par \uc2 Does this mean Japan was encouraged by the U.S. to end its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy emphasis? Not in the least. Snow is aware of the role \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  plays in keeping U.S. rates low (it buys U.S. Treasurys with proceeds from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ). So pulling a Y112 bid and re-establishing a Y111 bid would be acceptable in a smoothing role.  \par \par \uc2 -By David Gilmore, \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics; 860-767-9102; fxa@fxa.com  \par \par \uc2 (David Gilmore is an economist and partner at \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics, Essex, Conn., which advises institutional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market participants on a real-time basis. Neither the author nor \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics trades in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. Opinions expressed are those of the author, and not of Dow Jones Newswires.) [ 03-09-04 0800ET ]  \par \par \uc2 1057 4166  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040309e0390001m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pic