\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Settles Lower Against Yen, Euro, Pound  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jamie McGeever  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 799 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 22:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK -- The dollar weakened across the board Monday, particularly against the yen, after Japanese monetary authorities' bids for dollars via agent banks were apparently pulled.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar plunged around a full yen in a matter of minutes Monday afternoon to 111.15 yen when support at 112-112.10 yen, widely thought to have been provided by \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  bids, suddenly disappeared. This tripped huge stop-loss sell orders below 112 yen and sent the dollar tumbling.  \par \par \uc2 Before this though, the dollar had reached a fresh five-month high against the yen. But its sudden slide sent it lower against other main rivals and was easily the main talking point in a hitherto rangebound session almost devoid of other market-moving news.  \par \par \uc2 "They caught us all napping," said Grant Wilson, senior dealer at Mellon Bank in Pittsburgh, referring to the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Bank of Japan.  \par \par \uc2 Several traders said the ministry's bid for dollars just above 112 yen suddenly got pulled, causing stop-loss sell orders worth hundreds of millions of dollars below that level to run.  \par \par \uc2 "These guys aren't stupid," said Scott Ainsbury, portfolio manager at \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Concepts, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hedge fund in New York, referring to the Bank of Japan and \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "They would have heard about all the stops lying below 112 yen and it looks like they've just let the market clear itself out. I wouldn't be surprised to see them back in" buying dollars at these lower levels, he added.  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, traders later reported official support for the dollar emerging around 111.15 yen.  \par \par \uc2 But one trader at a Japanese bank in New York suggested the bids weren't pulled, but rather official buy orders were filled and for a few minutes, no new buy orders were placed.  \par \par \uc2 Mr. Wilson at Mellon Bank said the mechanics of the Bank of Japan and \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's tactics are largely irrelevant. The effect - burning speculators and keeping the market on its toes - is what matters. On both counts, they were successful Monday.  \par \par \uc2 "Bids were either pulled or filled, but once the big buyer of [dollars for yen] was no longer there, there was no one left to fill his shoes," Mr. Wilson said. "Once it [the bid] disappeared, all hell broke loose."  \par \par \uc2 In late trading Monday in New York, the dollar was trading at 111.16 yen versus 112.04 yen late Friday in New York and over a yen weaker than its new five-month peak of 112.35 yen hit earlier Monday.  \par \par \uc2 One of the main beneficiaries of the dollar's plunge against the yen was the euro, which moved up to $1.2404 from $1.2364, although it was dragged down against the yen to 137.83 yen from 138.57 yen late Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was down at 1.2745 francs from 1.2765 francs, while sterling was higher at $1.8490 from $1.8456.  \par \par \uc2 The apparent withdrawal of official Japanese bids for dollars came around an hour after U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow reiterated his view that the global financial system is more stable when \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  values are left to flexible, competitive markets.  \par \par \uc2 "We favor a strong dollar, but the relative value of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are best set through open and competitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets," Mr. Snow said following a speech to the National Association of State Treasurers.  \par \par \uc2 Mr. Snow said allowing market forces to work unfettered allows \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  values to send proper price signals that encourage optimal investment decisions.  \par \par \uc2 "Market forces help alleviate internal pressures that can build up," Mr. Snow said, adding that allowing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to adjust can also help the global economy work through imbalances.  \par \par \uc2 Friday, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets selling yen for dollars, traders said. It is estimated the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought as much as $20 billion - thought to be a record for one day - trying to keep the yen down amid a global selloff in the dollar following the weak U.S. employment report.  \par \par \uc2 The Labor Department said Friday only 21,000 nonfarm jobs were added in the U.S. economy last month, well below consensus forecasts of 125,000 and nowhere near enough to sustain the rapid economic growth in the U.S. What's more pressing for the dollar, however, is the jobs report's impact on monetary policy. With jobs growth so weak, the Federal Reserve looks like keeping interest rates low for longer than some had perhaps anticipated, thus ensuring the dollar's status as a low-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 -By Jamie McGeever; Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com [ 03-08-04 1659ET ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020040308e0380000p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
