\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Options: Dlr/Yen's New 5-Month Highs Support Vols  \b0\par\par\uc2 716 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 19:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Volatilities implied by dollar/yen \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options remain supported in New York on Monday as the spot rate hovers around fresh five-month highs, thanks to suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Having bought up to an estimated $20 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday, the BOJ is again bidding for dollars around Y112.00-112.10 Monday, dealers say.  \par \par \uc2 The monetary authorities' aggressive presence in the market recently has turned risk reversals - the premium investors are willing to pay for either "call" or "put" options - firmly in the dollar's favor. That is, the options market suggests investors are positioning themselves for further dollar strength against the yen rather than weakness.  \par \par \uc2 "Risk reversals are key to vols," said an options dealer in New York. In recent months, any spike higher in the dollar/yen spot rate has generally depressed vols because it went against the dollar's broader downtrend. Therefore, it took the spot rate back toward the middle of ranges and made new lows less and less likely.  \par \par \uc2 Now, however, it looks more likely that with the continued support of the BOJ and MOF, the dollar could push even higher and forge fresh multimonth highs. Dealers are therefore keen to buy upside protection.  \par \par \uc2 The options dealer in New York, for example, reported interest to buy two-week dollar/yen call options with a strike at Y112.50 and knock-out at Y111.95. With spot trading at just above Y112.10, this reflects the impact \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is having on investors.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers say the dollar's next target is Y112.50, then the Y113.57 area, the level just before the September meeting in Dubai of Group of Seven finance ministers and central bankers. Filling that gap would be a bullish signal for the dollar, which could then head as high as Y115.00, an unlikely target only three weeks ago when it was languishing at three-and-a-half-year lows just above Y105.00. Around 12:45 p.m. EST Monday, in the over-the-counter market, one-month at-the-money, or ATM, dollar/yen options implied volatility of 9.0%/9.3%, down slightly from 9.50%/9.70% earlier Monday in Tokyo and unchanged from New York Friday.  \par \par \uc2 One-month quarter delta risk-reversals favored dollar call/yen put options by 0.2%/0.5%, compared with 0.45%/0.65% in Tokyo Monday and 0.4%/0.7% in New York Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Volatilities implied by one-month ATM euro/dollar options were down at 11.40%/11.70% Monday in New York from 11.70%/12.05% in Tokyo Monday and 12.00%/12.20% in New York on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 They're coming off because the spot rate is trading in a narrow intraday range Monday and back around the middle of its broad range this year of $1.2060-$1.2930. One large Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reportedly bought a double-no-touch option last week worth more than $1 billion with knock-outs at $1.2080-$1.2680. At the moment, it looks like a good bet that that range will hold, say dealers.  \par \par \uc2 With the euro still well above Friday's levels following the weak U.S. employment report released that morning, one-month quarter delta risk-reversals favored euro calls/dollar puts by -0.1%/0.3%, only slightly down from 0.0%/0.4% Friday.  \par \par \uc2 One-month ATM euro/yen options implied volatility of 10.30%/10.55% Monday in New York, compared with 10.20%/10.60% in Tokyo Monday and 11.25%/12.25% in New York Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Around 12:45 p.m. EST Monday, the dollar was at Y112.12, up slightly from Y112.04 late Friday in New York but off its new five-month high of Y112.33 hit earlier Monday. The euro was at $1.2363, steady from $1.2364 late Friday in New York. It was also at Y138.62, up from Y138.57 Friday in New York.  \par \par \uc2 -By Jamie McGeever; Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com [ 08-03-04 1815GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 1077 4127 44427  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040308e038000s4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
