\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar stalls on fears Japan will step in again.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Naomi Tajitsu  \par \uc2 577 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 07:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 8 (Reuters) - The dollar steadied on Monday as traders grew wary that Japan might step into the market after the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  raced to a five-month high versus the yen during the previous session on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Market players were exasperated by the most recent indication that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "Nobody knows what's happening with the dollar/yen. Maybe you should ask the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  what's going on," said Kosuke Hanao, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at Royal Bank of Scotland.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar rose more than one yen on Friday to a high of 112.30 yen despite falling against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on surprisingly soft U.S. jobs data.  \par \par \uc2 At 0602 GMT, the dollar was at 112.07/12 yen, up only slightly from late U.S. levels on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 While the dollar climbed against the yen in the face of the jobs data, it was licking its wounds after taking a beating from other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The euro was at $1.2377/82 after surging 1.5 percent on Friday on figures that showed only 21,000 jobs were created last month, compared with an increase of 97,000 in January.  \par \par \uc2 The news drove the dollar down 1.3 percent to $1.2759 against the Swiss franc, while sterling rose around the same amount to $1.8458. Both \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  stayed near those levels on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said mixed messages were being sent to the market regarding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  other than the yen, with U.S. monetary authorities saying as late as last week that interest rates had to rise eventually.  \par \par \uc2 Since then, however, the dollar has weakened in light of the weak jobs report that dimmed prospects for an interest rate hike in the near future.  \par \par \uc2 "There's a mixed feeling in the market right now," said Hanao.  \par \par \uc2 "Traders want to sell dollars, but people like (Fed Chairman Alan) Greenspan and (U.S. Treasury Secretary John) Snow keep saying that we'll see a turnaround in the economy, so trading is likely to be range-bound for a while."  \par \par \uc2 STEPPING IN  \par \par \uc2 The yen, meanwhile, was being pressured by other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , hitting lows against the euro not seen since June and probing 5-= year troughs against sterling.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was hovering at 138.73/78 yen little changed after gaining 2.4 percent or about three yen on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Against sterling, the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trading at 207.09/19 yen.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's resilience against the yen once again points to possible massive dollar-buying by the authorities to buoy the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and protect Japan's export-led recovery.  \par \par \uc2 "They'll be stepping in whenever they feel necessary. They showed that after the jobs data came out," said Hanao, adding that Japan had no qualms about acting outside Tokyo trading hours.  \par \par \uc2 But some traders said such dollar-buying could not last.  \par \par \uc2 "I'm not convinced (the ministry) is going to continue doing this for several months coming," said Naomi Fink, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at BNP Paribas.  \par \par \uc2 "I think this is a trend that's particularly inherent ahead of the financial year-end. I don't think they can keep it up forever."  \par \par \uc2 The ministry has spent 10 trillion yen ($89.15 billion) to buy dollars so far this year, compared with 20 trillion yen for all of last year, a record in itself. ($1=112.16 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040309e038000w6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
